(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-14":{"number":14,"title":"Cutler","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 14 of 40","content":"Danse did not sleep after Fort Strong.\n\nI knew because I did not sleep either.\n\nThe Prydwen’s night cycle dimmed the lights but never silenced the ship. Engines thrummed through the deck. Pipes clicked. Boots passed in corridors. The Brotherhood could imitate darkness; it could not imitate rest.\n\nI found Danse in the maintenance bay at 0300 cleaning a weapon that was already clean.\n\nAt the other end of the bay, Haylen was checking the casualty supply ledger. She looked up when Danse set the rifle on the table for the third time.\n\n“Paladin, I need you to confirm two parts requests before the morning shift,” she said.\n\nHe reached for the ledger without looking at her. She kept her hand on it.\n\n“Read the quantities. Don't just sign.”\n\nDanse glanced up, irritated. I could see the argument ready on his tongue. Then he saw the two handwritten amendments in the margin.\n\n“Three filters?” he asked. “We only logged two damaged.”\n\n“The third came from the field kit you used at Fort Strong. It isn't on the return sheet.”\n\nHe stared at the page. The station breathed around us: engine vibration, the ticking of a cooling pipe, someone dragging a crate across the next deck.\n\n“Put it against my allocation,” he said.\n\n“It goes against the mission inventory,” Haylen replied. “You don't personally owe the Brotherhood every piece of equipment that wears out.”\n\nIt was such a small sentence. Danse looked as if she had taken away a rule he'd relied on for years.\n\nHaylen gave me a brief nod and went back to the radio desk. There was nothing tender about the exchange. She was exhausted, and she needed the records to be right. I admired her for insisting.\n\nDanse signed the correct line. When he finally put down his pen, there was a tremor at the base of his thumb.\n\n“You don't have to tell me about Cutler tonight,” I said.\n\n“I was not planning to.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHe took another breath, then one more. “The way Haylen said that—about the filter. Cutler used to argue with me about missing kit. He said I'd inventory the whole world if they gave me a large enough clipboard.”\n\nI smiled before I could help it. “Was he wrong?”\n\nDanse's mouth moved. Not quite a smile. “Not entirely.”\n\nHe told me no more that night. I sat on the workbench until the maintenance bay's lights changed for morning, close enough for him to know I hadn't gone, far enough to let the memory stay his.\n\n“You missed a spot,” I said.\n\nHe did not look up.\n\n“No, I didn’t.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nI sat on the bench opposite him.\n\nHe continued running cloth through the rifle barrel.\n\n“You don’t have to stay.”\n\n“Neither do you.”\n\n“I’m working.”\n\n“You’re polishing your emotions.”\n\nHis hands stopped.\n\n“That doesn’t make sense.”\n\n“It made sense in my head.”\n\n“That’s concerning.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe did not, but the tension eased.\n\nFor several minutes I watched him work.\n\nFinally he set the rifle down.\n\n“You want to know about Cutler.”\n\n“I want you to tell me whatever keeps you from dismantling that gun into atoms.”\n\nDanse leaned back.\n\n“The first time I met him, he tried to sell me my own scrap.”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“In Rivet City. I had a box of servo parts beside my stand. Walked away for maybe two minutes. When I came back, Cutler was trying to sell them to a customer.”\n\n“That’s incredible.”\n\n“I nearly broke his nose.”\n\n“Also incredible.”\n\n“He said if I left valuable inventory unattended, I deserved to lose it.”\n\n“What happened?”\n\n“I made him give me half the caps.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse smiled down at his hands.\n\n“We became partners three days later.”\n\nThe story came slowly after that.\n\nRivet City markets. Junk runs through dangerous buildings. Splitting food when one of them had a bad week. Competing over who could find the strangest pre-War object and convincing tourists it had value.\n\n“Cutler once sold a broken hand dryer as a ‘portable wind generator.’”\n\n“Did it work?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did the customer know?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“Your Brotherhood origin story involves fraud.”\n\n“Minor misrepresentation.”\n\nI laughed so hard someone in the next bay told us to shut up.\n\nDanse looked younger when he spoke about those years.\n\nThen the story reached recruitment.\n\n“The Brotherhood came through Rivet City looking for people who could handle themselves. Cutler heard ‘regular meals’ and decided immediately.”\n\n“And you?”\n\n“I heard ‘training.’”\n\n“Of course you did.”\n\n“We thought we had nothing to lose.”\n\nHis smile faded.\n\n“That’s usually when people discover how much they had.”\n\nThe maintenance bay seemed colder.\n\nDanse stared at the rifle on the table.\n\n“Years later, when I found him in that nest, he looked at me.”\n\nI did not move.\n\n“His face was changing. His voice…” Danse swallowed. “I don’t know if he knew me.”\n\n“You said he didn’t attack.”\n\n“He didn’t.”\n\n“Did he speak?”\n\n“Not clearly.”\n\nDanse rubbed both hands over his face.\n\n“I fired before he finished changing.”\n\nThe sentence came out broken around the edges.\n\nI moved to the bench beside him.\n\nNot touching.\n\nClose.\n\n“I told myself it was mercy.”\n\n“Maybe it was.”\n\n“I told myself there was no person left to save.”\n\n“Maybe there wasn’t.”\n\nHe looked at me, frustrated.\n\n“You keep saying maybe.”\n\n“Because certainty would be a lie.”\n\nHis anger collapsed.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe looked down.\n\n“That’s what I hate.”\n\n“The not knowing?”\n\n“The possibility that I could be wrong and still have done something irreversible.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nNot hatred of mutants.\n\nFear of being the person who obeyed the rule and killed his friend anyway.\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\nThis time I did it slowly enough that he could move away.\n\nHe did not.\n\nMy fingers closed around his.\n\nDanse stared at them.\n\n“I don’t know what Cutler was in those last minutes,” I said. “But I know what he was before. Your friend. The person who knew you before the armor. Remembering that doesn’t betray the Brotherhood.”\n\nHis thumb shifted against mine.\n\n“No.”\n\n“And questioning what happened doesn’t either.”\n\n“That part is debatable.”\n\n“Then debate me.”\n\nHis mouth twisted.\n\n“At three in the morning?”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHe gave me a tired, real smile.\n\nOur hands were still joined.\n\nNeither of us acknowledged it.\n\nEventually Danse said, “He would have made fun of me.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“This.”\n\nHe lifted our hands slightly.\n\nMy heart tripped.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I’m terrible at this.”\n\n“At holding hands?”\n\n“At…”\n\nHe gestured vaguely between us with his free hand.\n\n“This.”\n\nI decided mercy was appropriate.\n\n“Talking?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Terrible.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nI started to pull away.\n\nHis fingers tightened.\n\nJust for a second.\n\nThen he released me.\n\nThe absence of contact felt louder than it should have.\n\nDanse picked up the rifle again.\n\nHe did not resume cleaning it.\n\n“I haven’t told anyone that much about him.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“Because you looked like it hurt.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen nodded once.\n\n“Get some sleep.”\n\n“That sounds like an order.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“You coming?”\n\nThe words left my mouth before I considered them.\n\nDanse’s eyes widened.\n\nI felt heat climb my face.\n\n“To the sleeping quarters,” I added quickly. “To sleep. Separately.”\n\n“Yes.” His voice cracked slightly. He cleared his throat. “Obviously.”\n\nWe walked back together without speaking.\n\nAt my bunk, he paused.\n\n“Good night.”\n\n“Good night, Danse.”\n\nHe started away.\n\nI lay down beneath the pipes.\n\nTen seconds later, from the corridor, I heard him mutter, “Outstanding.”\n\nI buried my face in the blanket so no one would hear me laugh.\n\nThe next morning I found him in the Prydwen’s mess with coffee so black it looked structurally unsound.\n\nHe had told me about Cutler during the night: Rivet City, the Brotherhood, the patrol, the super mutants, the thing he had been forced to do when the man he loved like a brother was no longer entirely the man he remembered.\n\nNow Danse stared into the cup like the answer might be at the bottom.\n\nI sat across from him.\n\n“Did you sleep?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\n“Approximately forty minutes.”\n\n“That is not sleep. That is losing an argument with consciousness.”\n\nHe made a sound that might have been amusement.\n\nI took the coffee from his hand and tasted it.\n\nMy entire mouth objected.\n\n“What is wrong with you?”\n\nDanse looked genuinely confused.\n\n“It’s coffee.”\n\n“It’s a chemical weapon.”\n\n“It’s efficient.”\n\n“Food should not be evaluated by tactical efficiency.”\n\n“Coffee is not food.”\n\n“That is the first sensible thing you’ve said about it.”\n\nHe took the cup back.\n\nThe absurdity helped.\n\nFor a while.\n\nThen he said, “I should have found them sooner.”\n\nThere it was.\n\n“You told me the patrol disappeared in an irradiated area full of super mutants.”\n\n“I should have anticipated the route.”\n\n“You weren’t even commanding the mission.”\n\n“I was his friend.”\n\nThe sentence cracked in the middle.\n\nDanse looked away quickly.\n\nI did not tell him it was not his fault.\n\nHe would have rejected that because grief is not a courtroom and guilt does not care about evidence.\n\nInstead I asked, “What would Cutler say if he heard you taking responsibility for the Commonwealth existing around him?”\n\nDanse’s mouth tightened.\n\n“He’d tell me to stop being an idiot.”\n\n“Sounds wise.”\n\n“He was not.”\n\nThe smile came anyway.\n\n“What was he like before?”\n\nDanse leaned back.\n\n“Reckless.”\n\n“Compared to you, a chair is reckless.”\n\n“He cheated at target competitions.”\n\n“How?”\n\n“He’d adjust the sights on my rifle when I wasn’t looking.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse did too.\n\nThen his eyes turned wet.\n\nHe looked furious about it.\n\n“I remember the last time I saw him as himself more clearly than I remember some entire years of my life.”\n\nI reached across the table.\n\nMy hand stopped halfway.\n\nChoice.\n\nDanse looked at it.\n\nThen placed his hand in mine.\n\nHis grip was careful at first.\n\nThen not.\n\n“I’m afraid,” he said quietly, “that if I let myself care about people the way I cared about him, I’ll lose them the same way.”\n\n“That’s possible.”\n\nHe looked down at our hands as if he had only just realized he was still holding mine.\n\n“Cutler used to say I could turn anything into an assignment,” he said.\n\n“Was he wrong?”\n\n“No.” A faint smile. “He once ordered me to take an afternoon off.”\n\n“That is deeply funny.”\n\n“I told him he lacked authority.”\n\n“What did he do?”\n\n“Stole my boots.”\n\nI laughed so suddenly a nearby knight glanced over.\n\nDanse’s smile widened before grief pulled at it.\n\n“I found them hanging from a pipe in Rivet City.”\n\nThe memory hurt him.\n\nIt also warmed him.\n\nFor once I did not try to separate those things.\n\nHe flinched at the answer.\n\nI held on.\n\n“I’m not going to promise the Commonwealth can’t take people. It can. You know that better than I do.”\n\n“Then why—”\n\n“Because not caring doesn’t save them.”\n\nHis gaze dropped to our joined hands.\n\n“It only makes you lose them early.”\n\nDanse went very still.\n\nAround us, soldiers ate breakfast, complained about duty shifts, and argued about ammunition requisitions. Life continued with rude efficiency.\n\nFinally his thumb moved across my knuckles.\n\n“You have an irritating habit of making arguments I can’t easily dismiss.”\n\n“You could try bad coffee.”\n\n“That is not an argument.”\n\n“Neither is this beverage.”\n\nThe laugh that came out of him was quiet and tired and real.\n\nHe did not release my hand until his coffee had gone cold.\n","word_count":1918},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-17":{"number":17,"title":"Benchwork","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 17 of 40","content":"Danse flirted like a man attempting to repair a radio with a combat knife.\n\nThat is to say: earnestly, dangerously, and without recognizing what he was doing.\n\nWe were at a weapon bench on the Prydwen when it started.\n\nI had Righteous Authority disassembled for another adjustment. Danse stood beside me sorting components.\n\n“You’ve improved,” he said.\n\n“At weapon modification?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You no longer install parts backward.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“You almost complimented me without insult.”\n\n“That wasn’t an insult. It was an observation.”\n\n“Your romance technique must be devastating.”\n\nThe component in his hand stopped moving.\n\nI realized what I had said.\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“No. You said something about…”\n\n“I was joking.”\n\nHis ears began to turn red.\n\n“Right.”\n\nI returned my attention to the rifle with the concentration of someone defusing a bomb.\n\nDanse did the same.\n\nFor approximately thirty seconds.\n\nThen he said, “For the record, I’m not attempting to romance you.”\n\nI nearly dropped the barrel.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Obviously.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nSilence.\n\nA scribe at the next bench quietly gathered her tools and left.\n\nCoward.\n\nDanse cleared his throat.\n\n“I only meant that your technical skills have improved.”\n\n“Understood.”\n\n“And I enjoy working with you.”\n\nMy hands stopped.\n\nHe seemed not to notice the second sentence was far more dangerous than the first.\n\n“I enjoy working with you too.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nAnother silence.\n\nHe picked up the wrong screw.\n\nI stared.\n\nDanse looked down, realized it, and set it back.\n\n“Don’t,” he said.\n\n“I did not say a word.”\n\n“You were going to.”\n\n“I notice patterns.”\n\nHe gave me a look that should have melted armor.\n\nInstead it made warmth spread through my chest.\n\nWe were on our way out of the maintenance bay when Haylen intercepted us with a clipboard, a crate, and the expression of somebody who had been told to ignore an obvious problem.\n\n\"The replacement reflex sight came without a calibration tag,\" she said. \"Supply wants us to sign it into service anyway.\"\n\n\"Probably works,\" the quartermaster called from his desk.\n\nDanse set his kit down, verified the rifle was clear, and examined the mounting points without touching the trigger. \"Probably is not a certification standard.\"\n\nHaylen read off two serial numbers. The shipment number belonged to another crate; the part itself had no record of having been tested.\n\n\"Two discrepancies,\" she said.\n\n\"At least two,\" Danse answered, \"and I will not create a third by signing a false inspection.\"\n\nThe quartermaster muttered something about being short of parts. Danse wrote SHORTAGE on the release form, signed a hold order, and returned the sight to its box.\n\nI watched him cap his pen. \"You know, a man who takes paperwork that seriously is dangerously attractive.\"\n\nHaylen made a sound that might have been a cough.\n\n\"Procedure is not a romantic gesture,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's devastating news. Here I was, about to swoon over serial-number verification.\"\n\nHe gave me a look of strict disapproval, ruined by the slightest movement at the corner of his mouth. Haylen took the clipboard away before either of us could say something worse.\n\nThe other soldiers would never know we had delayed our evening to keep an untested part out of their hands. I liked that he had done it anyway. I liked even more that he had listened when Haylen brought him evidence, rather than treating rank as a substitute for being right.\n\nThat evening we took two beers to the observation deck.\n\nDanse had called it “decompression after duty.”\n\nI called it having a drink together.\n\nHe refused to call it anything.\n\nThe Commonwealth below was scattered with lights: Diamond City, settlements, campfires, places where people had decided darkness did not get everything.\n\nDanse leaned on the rail.\n\n“You ever think about leaving?” I asked.\n\n“The Brotherhood?”\n\n“The Commonwealth.”\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n“Where would I go?”\n\n“Anywhere.”\n\n“That’s not a destination.”\n\n“Neither is purpose.”\n\nHe took a drink.\n\n“You really dislike that word.”\n\n“I dislike when people use it instead of wanting something.”\n\n“Wanting isn’t always useful.”\n\n“Neither is beer.”\n\n“That’s debatable.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse’s smile stayed after mine faded.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You were looking at me.”\n\n“I do that sometimes.”\n\nMy own words, returned.\n\nThe air between us changed.\n\nNeither of us looked away.\n\nDanse’s face was different when he was not actively trying to be a paladin. Softer around the eyes. Younger. There was a small scar near one eyebrow I had never asked about. His mouth was more expressive than he seemed to realize.\n\nI became extremely aware of it.\n\nHe looked at mine too.\n\nThen a vertibird roared past the deck.\n\nThe moment broke.\n\nDanse looked over the rail.\n\n“I should check the duty roster.”\n\n“At twenty-two hundred?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“You heard me.”\n\n“I am not a coward.”\n\n“Then stay.”\n\nI expected him to leave out of principle.\n\nInstead he sat on the bench beside me.\n\n“Fine.”\n\n“Outstanding.”\n\n“Don’t start.”\n\nWe stayed.\n\nThe conversation wandered into meaningless things, which somehow felt more intimate than confession.\n\nWorst pre-War food we had eaten. Danse claimed canned imitation crab was a crime against humanity. I argued that the Brotherhood should revise its mission statement accordingly.\n\nFavorite weapon modifications. Best places to sleep in the field. Whether a mirelurk counted as seafood.\n\n“It lives in the water,” I said.\n\n“So do things I would not eat.”\n\n“That is cowardice again.”\n\n“That word does not mean what you think it means.”\n\nAt some point our knees touched.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nThe contact lasted long enough to become intentional.\n\nDanse looked down.\n\nThen at me.\n\n“I meant what I said earlier.”\n\n“About not romancing me?”\n\nHe shut his eyes briefly.\n\n“No. About enjoying working with you.”\n\n“Oh.”\n\nHis voice lowered.\n\n“It’s… comfortable.”\n\nFor Danse, that might have been a declaration.\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“I feel the same.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\nOur knees remained together.\n\nNo kiss.\n\nNo dramatic confession.\n\nJust the Prydwen humming around us and a soldier who feared attachment allowing one small point of contact to remain.\n\nEventually Danse stood.\n\n“Get some sleep.”\n\n“There’s the romance technique.”\n\nHe groaned.\n\nI grinned.\n\nAs he walked away he called back, “Good night.”\n\nNot civilian.\n\nNot soldier.\n\nJust good night.\n\nI sat there another minute smiling into my beer like an idiot.\n\nHis next attempt at flirting involved a torque wrench.\n\nI was replacing the recoil compensator on a combat rifle when Danse appeared at the bench and set a tool beside my elbow.\n\n“You’ll get better leverage with this.”\n\nI looked at the wrench.\n\nThen at him.\n\n“Did you come all the way across the Prydwen to critique my leverage?”\n\n“I was passing through.”\n\n“You are carrying two cups of coffee.”\n\nHe looked down as if surprised to find them.\n\n“One is for you.”\n\n“Is it drinkable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“That was not a confident answer.”\n\n“I added sugar.”\n\n“How much?”\n\nDanse became suspiciously interested in the rifle.\n\nI tasted it.\n\nThe coffee was still strong enough to strip paint, but someone had clearly attempted mercy.\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“You remembered how I take it.”\n\nHis ears reddened.\n\n“I remember operational preferences.”\n\n“Sugar is tactical now?”\n\n“It affects morale.”\n\nI laughed so hard I had to set the cup down.\n\nDanse crossed his arms.\n\n“I fail to see what’s amusing.”\n\n“You have been flirting with me for weeks and somehow you’re doing it through logistics.”\n\nHis entire face stopped.\n\nI stopped laughing.\n\nOh.\n\nThere are moments when the Commonwealth seems to hold its breath just to see what stupid thing I will say next.\n\nDanse stared at me.\n\n“I have not—”\n\n“You don’t have to—”\n\n“I mean, I wasn’t—”\n\n“Right.”\n\n“Not intentionally.”\n\nThat shut us both up.\n\nHis eyes widened a fraction as he heard himself.\n\nI leaned against the bench.\n\n“Not intentionally?”\n\nDanse looked as though he would have preferred another super mutant battle.\n\n“I enjoy your company.”\n\nThe words emerged with the rigid precision of a formal report.\n\n“I had gathered that.”\n\n“I respect your judgment.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“Frequently.”\n\n“Generous.”\n\n“And I…”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nHis gaze dropped to the coffee cup.\n\n“I notice when you’re not around.”\n\nThe room suddenly felt too small.\n\nI let the teasing fall away.\n\n“I notice too.”\n\nDanse looked up.\n\nWe stood on opposite sides of a workbench covered in springs, screws, and a half-disassembled rifle.\n\nIt was not romantic by any reasonable standard.\n\nIt felt more intimate than a candlelit dinner could have.\n\nHe reached toward me, hesitated, then picked up the torque wrench instead.\n\n“This fastener is going to strip.”\n\nBefore he could escape into the fastener, I noticed a small cloth-wrapped bundle near his elbow.\n\n“What’s that?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Suspicious answer.”\n\n“It’s a component.”\n\n“For?”\n\nDanse hesitated, then unfolded the cloth. Inside was a polished beam focuser, cleaner than anything available in the common parts bins.\n\nI stared at it.\n\n“You requisitioned this.”\n\n“I located it.”\n\n“With paperwork?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“For my rifle.”\n\n“For Righteous Authority.”\n\n“My rifle.”\n\nHe folded his arms. “You’ve been complaining about energy dispersion.”\n\n“I complained once.”\n\n“You were correct.”\n\nI picked up the focuser. “Is this a gift?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“It improves mission effectiveness.”\n\n“Is that what Brotherhood officers call flowers?”\n\nHis entire face turned red.\n\n“I have never given anyone flowers.”\n\n“I believe you.”\n\nHe reached for the part. I pulled it out of range.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nThe embarrassment eased enough for him to meet my eyes.\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nThere it was again. Care translated into equipment because equipment had specifications and affection did not.\n\nI could work with that.\n\nI installed the new focuser while Danse watched over my shoulder.\n\n“Quarter turn,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re over-tightening it.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\nHe reached around me and put his hand over mine on the tool.\n\nThe correction became unnecessary almost immediately.\n\nNeither of us moved.\n\nHis chest was warm against my back. His breath caught near my ear. For a man who could issue orders under gunfire without hesitation, Danse had no procedure for standing too close to someone he wanted.\n\n“Quarter turn?” I asked quietly.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You stopped turning.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\nHis hand left mine with visible reluctance.\n\nThe rifle, at least, had the decency to remain uncomplicated.\n\nI stared.\n\nHe stared back.\n\n“That’s your retreat?”\n\n“I don’t know what you mean.”\n\n“Coward.”\n\nHis mouth opened.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\nActually laughed, head dropping for a second as the sound escaped him.\n\nIt was warm and rough and so rare I forgot the comeback I had prepared.\n\nWhen he recovered, he slid the wrench toward me.\n\n“Use the correct tool.”\n\nI took it.\n\nOur fingers brushed.\n\nNeither of us pretended not to notice.\n\nThe rifle took another twenty minutes.\n\nThe conversation took considerably longer.\n\nThree days after the incident with the wrench, Danse found me sorting ammunition at the workbench.\n\n\"I have a question,\" he said.\n\n\"Is it a test?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Are you certain? Because with you those categories overlap.\"\n\nHe set a small box of cleaned bolts beside me. He had labeled them by size and condition.\n\nThe case latch caught as I tried to slide it into place. Danse noticed before I swore at it.\n\n“Stop. The hinge is bent.”\n\n“I'm beginning to think the entire Commonwealth is bent.”\n\n“That is not an adequate inspection category.”\n\nHe took the case, checked the latch, then set it aside with a scrap of red cloth tied around its handle. He didn't try to force it shut. Every mistake at a bench had consequences, and I liked that he remembered that even when he was flustered.\n\n“Should I fetch another?” I asked.\n\n“Please.”\n\nI stopped moving. He noticed.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You said please.”\n\n“I am capable of courtesy.”\n\n“Right, but you usually issue it at parade-ground volume.”\n\nHis ears colored. “You are making this unnecessarily difficult.”\n\n“Only because you're worth annoying.”\n\nDanse opened his mouth, closed it, and checked the safe case twice before he spoke again. The thoroughness was familiar; the uncertainty was not.\n\n“I wasn't trained for conversations in which the answer cannot be evaluated against a written standard,” he admitted.\n\n“Most of us aren't.”\n\n“Then I would prefer to ask plainly, even if the answer is complicated.”\n\nHe set the cartridges aside and faced me with a gravity that might have been ridiculous if it weren't so sincere. I waited for the question. I knew whatever came next would sound like Danse, not like someone serenading me or handing me a parable about love.\n\n\"Are you currently involved with anyone?\"\n\nI nearly dropped a cartridge.\n\n\"That's direct.\"\n\n\"I thought you preferred directness.\"\n\n\"I do.\" I set the cartridge down. \"No, not at the moment. But there's something you should know if this is going where I think it might.\"\n\nThe tips of his ears turned red. \"And where is that?\"\n\n\"Somewhere with far more paperwork than either of us expected.\"\n\nHe folded his arms.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. For me that means I might have more than one loving relationship, with everyone fully informed and consenting. No hidden partners. No rules made behind somebody's back. And it means you would always get to decide what you were comfortable with.\"\n\nDanse stared at the box of bolts.\n\n\"I understand the definition,\" he said. \"I'm considering the implications.\"\n\n\"That is an extremely Danse answer.\"\n\n\"Would you expect me to share information about our relationship with other people?\"\n\n\"Not your private history. We would decide together what stays between us, what gets disclosed to someone directly affected, and what is nobody else's business. I won't share your trauma as a relationship update.\"\n\nHe gave that the thought it deserved.\n\n\"Would I be required to pursue other relationships?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Would you expect me not to experience jealousy?\"\n\n\"Also no. I'd expect you to talk to me rather than turning it into an order.\"\n\nHe looked almost offended. \"I don't issue orders about personal matters.\"\n\n\"You told me to eat lunch yesterday.\"\n\n\"That was a health recommendation.\"\n\n\"At parade-ground volume.\"\n\nA reluctant breath escaped him. It might have been a laugh.\n\n\"I was taught loyalty is singular,\" he said. \"One chain of command. One mission. One oath. I need to think about whether I've mistaken those rules for rules about people.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to think.\"\n\n\"And to say no?\"\n\n\"Yes. Even to me.\"\n\nHe nodded. \"Then I would like to keep seeing where this leads. I won't ask you to promise exclusivity. If my feelings change, I'll tell you rather than pretend.\"\n\nI felt the relief like someone had loosened a strap I'd forgotten was tight.\n\n\"One request,\" he said.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"No critical relationship discussions while handling live ammunition.\"\n\n\"Fair.\"\n\nHe slid the ammunition case out of my reach. Then, very carefully, he placed his hand on the bench beside mine. An invitation, not a command. I let our little fingers touch.\n\nThe next time he told me to use the correct tool, I made a point of asking whether he had a form for romantic incompetence.\n\nHe said, \"Several.\"\n\nI believed him.","word_count":2517},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-32":{"number":32,"title":"Civilian Clothes","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 32 of 40","content":"Danse owned exactly one outfit that was not Brotherhood-issued.\n\nIt was ugly.\n\nI loved it immediately.\n\nHe emerged from the back room at Bravo wearing faded work pants, a brown shirt, and a pre-War jacket we had traded for at Bunker Hill. Without the uniform, without armor, without rank insignia, he looked like an entirely different man.\n\nHe looked deeply uncomfortable.\n\n“Well?” he asked.\n\nI stared.\n\n“That bad?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You’re staring.”\n\n“I do that sometimes.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You’re enjoying this.”\n\n“Immensely.”\n\nHe looked down at himself.\n\n“The jacket is impractical.”\n\n“It has pockets.”\n\n“Not enough.”\n\n“No garment has enough pockets for you.”\n\n“That’s a design failure.”\n\nWe were going to Diamond City.\n\nDanse had resisted for days because the Brotherhood still operated nearby and because being recognized felt dangerous.\n\nBut Bravo needed supplies. More importantly, Danse needed to walk through a place where nobody cared about his former rank.\n\nThe market was crowded.\n\nHe stayed close.\n\nNot protectively this time.\n\nUneasily.\n\nPeople bumped him without apology. Vendors yelled prices. A child nearly ran into him carrying noodles.\n\nDanse looked more overwhelmed than he had during some firefights.\n\n“You all right?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Liar.”\n\n“I’m adjusting.”\n\n“To civilians?”\n\n“To not having a visible role.”\n\nThat made sense.\n\nA Brotherhood uniform told people what he was before he spoke.\n\nNow he had to be only himself.\n\nWe bought ammunition, soap, coffee, and a new pan.\n\nAt the kitchenware stall Danse checked the handle of the pan twice, then asked the woman selling it whether she could show him the other side of the hinge.\n\n“You think I'm trying to cheat you?” she demanded.\n\n“No, ma'am. I think the rivet may be loose.”\n\nShe turned the pan over, squinted, and gave it a hard shake. The handle moved.\n\n“Well, damn.” She dug another pan out of a crate. “You want the good one or a discount on the bad one?”\n\n“The good one.”\n\n“Then stop looking like I'm charging you for a suit of power armor. That's six caps.”\n\nHe counted them carefully. When she held out her hand for the payment, he hesitated. I could see him measuring his voice, looking for the rank or designation that would make an ordinary transaction feel safer.\n\n“Thank you,” he said finally.\n\n“Enjoy your breakfast, whatever it is.”\n\nWe were halfway down the market aisle before he spoke again.\n\n“I nearly told her to inspect the rest of the stock.”\n\n“You would've been right about the rivet.”\n\n“That doesn't entitle me to command her shop.”\n\n“Nope.”\n\nHe seemed disappointed and oddly proud of the observation at the same time.\n\nA few stalls later I reached for a scarf from a stack of woolens. Danse steadied the display before it could fall. His arm brushed the small of my back, deliberate enough that I knew he hadn't needed to reach quite so far.\n\n“Careful,” he murmured.\n\n“Is that an order?”\n\n“An assessment.”\n\n“Of the scarves?”\n\nHe looked at my mouth and then away. “Of the situation.”\n\nMy pulse did a thoroughly embarrassing thing. He cleared his throat and began examining the stitchwork with the gravity of a field inspection. I let him, because I wanted to see how long he could keep pretending he hadn't been flirting.\n\nAt a clothing stall, Danse stopped beside a dark red scarf.\n\nI saw the look.\n\n“You like it.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You absolutely do.”\n\n“It serves no purpose.”\n\n“It keeps your neck warm.”\n\n“My jacket does that.”\n\n“Then it serves a second purpose.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Looking good.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nI bought it.\n\nDanse protested for six straight minutes.\n\nThen wore it all the way home.\n\nAt Bravo, he stood in front of the cracked mirror adjusting the knot.\n\n“You’re smiling,” I said.\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“You are.”\n\n“It’s functional.”\n\n“It’s red.”\n\n“That is a color.”\n\n“It’s your color now.”\n\nHe looked at my reflection in the mirror.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That’s suspicious.”\n\nI stepped behind him and fixed the scarf where it had folded under the jacket collar.\n\nMy fingers brushed the side of his neck.\n\nDanse went still.\n\nThe mirror caught his eyes meeting mine.\n\nThe room changed.\n\nI let my hand remain there.\n\nHe covered it with his.\n\n“You know,” I said softly, “you’re allowed to keep things because you like them.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re allowed to look good because you want to.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You’re allowed—”\n\nDanse turned and kissed me before I could finish.\n\nIt was not careful like the first time.\n\nStill gentle.\n\nBut no hesitation.\n\nHis hands settled at my waist. Mine caught in the front of his ridiculous civilian jacket.\n\nWhen we separated, I was breathing harder.\n\nDanse looked startled by himself.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe blinked.\n\n“Right. No apologizing.”\n\n“Correct.”\n\nHis forehead rested against mine.\n\n“I like the scarf,” he admitted.\n\nI laughed.\n\n“That was harder to say than kissing me, wasn’t it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Hopeless.”\n\n“Apparently.”\n\nThe next morning he wore it again.\n\nHe pretended that meant nothing.\n\nSo did I.\n\nWe were both lying.\n\nDiamond City recognized him eventually.\n\nNot everyone.\n\nOne Brotherhood caravan guard did.\n\nDanse and I were leaving the market with a bag of coffee beans and two replacement fuses when the guard stepped from beside the gate.\n\nHis eyes moved from the red scarf to Danse’s face.\n\n“Paladin?”\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nThe title hit him like a hand against the chest.\n\nThe guard went pale as recognition became memory.\n\n“Sir, I—”\n\nDanse’s hand moved slightly toward mine, then stopped before touching.\n\n“I’m not a paladin.”\n\nThe guard looked around as if saying the wrong thing might summon Maxson.\n\n“I heard you were dead.”\n\n“So did I.”\n\nIt was the driest thing Danse had ever said.\n\nI nearly choked.\n\nThe guard did not know whether he was allowed to laugh.\n\nDanse saved him.\n\n“You should return to your caravan.”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nHe took one step, stopped.\n\n“Sorry. Not sir.”\n\nDanse’s expression softened by a fraction.\n\n“Be safe.”\n\nThe guard nodded and left.\n\nWe made it outside the gate before Danse sat on a concrete barrier.\n\nI put the supplies down beside him.\n\n“You okay?”\n\n“No.”\n\nGood answer.\n\nI waited.\n\n“He looked afraid of me.”\n\n“He looked afraid of the situation.”\n\n“I used to inspect his unit.”\n\n“You remember him?”\n\n“Knight Vargas. Poor trigger discipline when he was nervous. Improved after his second deployment.”\n\nOf course Danse remembered.\n\n“He probably thought I was going to arrest him for talking to me.”\n\n“You are currently wearing a scarf I bought because it was pretty.”\n\n“That does not eliminate the possibility of arrest.”\n\n“It substantially lowers the intimidation factor.”\n\nHe looked down at the scarf.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThen he went quiet again.\n\n“I wanted to ask how the Prydwen is.”\n\n“Why didn’t you?”\n\n“Because he would have had to choose between answering me and following his orders.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n“That was kind.”\n\n“It was practical.”\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse gave me a sideways look.\n\n“Still doing that?” I asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Renaming kindness so it doesn’t embarrass you.”\n\nHe looked back toward the city gate.\n\n“Maybe.”\n\nWe stayed until the moment passed.\n\nThen I picked up the bag.\n\n“Come on. We still need soap.”\n\nDanse stood.\n\n“Two bars.”\n\n“Three.”\n\n“Why three?”\n\n“Because normal people stock extras.”\n\n“We are not normal.”\n\n“Fair.”\n\nAt the clothing stall, the same vendor who had sold me the scarf waved us over.\n\n“Got something for your man.”\n\nDanse visibly regretted having a face.\n\n“My—”\n\nThe vendor held up a dark wool sweater.\n\n“Warm. No insignia. Actual sleeves.”\n\nI touched the fabric.\n\nDanse checked the seams.\n\nOf course he did.\n\n“How much?” he asked.\n\nThe vendor named a price.\n\nDanse negotiated with ruthless precision until she called him a bastard and reduced it by fifteen caps.\n\nHe wore the sweater home.\n\nHalfway to Bravo, rain started.\n\nHe pulled the red scarf higher around his neck.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“What?”\n\n“You look like a person going home from the market.”\n\nHis brow furrowed.\n\n“I am a person going home from the market.”\n\nExactly.\n\nHe realized what he had said a few steps later.\n\nHis pace slowed.\n\nI did not comment.\n\nNeither did he.\n\nBut his hand found mine.\n\nThe trip home took longer because Danse kept discovering that civilian clothing had consequences.\n\nThe jacket caught on a rusted fence.\n\nThe work pants lacked reinforced knees.\n\nThe sweater was, according to him, “thermally adequate but tactically irresponsible.”\n\n“You bought it.”\n\n“I was cold.”\n\n“You negotiated fifteen caps off it like a military campaign.”\n\n“Overpricing should be challenged.”\n\nAt a roadside checkpoint run by settlers, nobody saluted him.\n\nNobody called him Paladin.\n\nA guard simply glanced at our bags and said, “Evening, Danse.”\n\nHe stopped walking.\n\nThe guard looked worried. “Did I get the name wrong?”\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse’s answer came slowly.\n\n“No. You didn’t.”\n\nWe continued down the road.\n\nA few minutes later he touched the red scarf at his throat.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You look like you’re thinking hard enough to damage the sweater.”\n\nHe ignored that.\n\n“It was strange.”\n\n“Not being called Paladin?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Bad?”\n\nHe took several steps before answering.\n\n“No.”\n\nThat single word seemed to surprise him more than it surprised me.\n\nAt Bravo, Danse emptied his pockets onto the table.\n\nTwo fuses. A screwdriver. Three screws he had apparently acquired without noticing. A folded market receipt. One bottle cap flattened almost perfectly round.\n\nI picked it up.\n\n“What’s this?”\n\n“Trash.”\n\n“You carried trash home?”\n\nHe took it from me, then hesitated.\n\n“A child at the gate gave it to me.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“He said it was lucky.”\n\nI waited.\n\nDanse stared at the cap.\n\n“You kept it.”\n\n“It was in my pocket.”\n\n“You put it there.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe set it carefully on the shelf beside the detective novel.\n\nThe man who once catalogued only equipment had brought home a useless piece of metal because a child thought it mattered.\n\nI decided not to tease him.\n\nThat was how much I loved him.\n\nBefore putting the clothes away, he caught his reflection in the cracked bunker mirror.\n\nNo armor. No insignia. Red scarf, ugly sweater, tired eyes.\n\nHe studied himself longer than usual.\n\n“Recognize him?” I asked.\n\n“Not entirely.”\n\n“Do you have to?”\n\nHe considered.\n\n“No.”\n\nThen he smiled at the reflection, faintly, like an introduction.\n\nThe mirror did not give him an answer. It only gave him a face he was learning to claim without a uniform telling him what it meant.\n\nThat night he folded the sweater and placed it in the same drawer as his Brotherhood undersuit.\n\nOld uniform beneath.\n\nCivilian clothes above.\n\nNot replacement.\n\nLayering.\n\nI watched him close the drawer.\n\n“Red tomorrow?” I asked.\n\n“The scarf is useful.”\n\n“Sure.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe sighed.\n\n“I like it.”\n\n“Say the whole thing.”\n\n“I like the red scarf you bought me because it looks good.”\n\nI put a hand to my chest.\n\n“Growth.”\n\nHe kissed me before I could get worse.\n\nI considered this positive reinforcement.\n","word_count":1822},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-42":{"number":42,"title":"Letter from the Old World","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass two","content":"The envelope was made from a page torn out of an old technical manual.\n\nIt arrived at Brasswatch Yard wrapped around a small packet of electrical connectors, delivered by a trader who did not know the sender's name. The paper had been folded so precisely that I could almost hear Danse correcting someone's technique.\n\nHe recognized the handwriting before he opened it.\n\nHis face went still.\n\n\"Haylen?\"\n\nI looked at the packet, then at him.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nHe carried it into the workshop and shut the door. I stayed outside. It was not an act of generosity; it was an effort not to confuse my curiosity with his permission.\n\nFive minutes passed.\n\nThen ten.\n\nAt last, he opened the door.\n\nDanse kept the letter in his right hand. I could see the pressure of his thumb where the paper folded; I couldn't see what Haylen had written, and I didn't try.\n\n\"The connectors are the correct gauge for the purifier,\" I said, pointing to the parts visible on the bench. \"We can check those without discussing the letter.\"\n\n\"I am aware.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nHe stayed in the doorway long enough that I wondered whether he expected an argument. Then he stepped aside and let me reach the service log.\n\nAster stopped by with a sample tray and a schedule from the market committee. The people using Brasswatch water had questions about the flow rate, not the contents of a former Brotherhood paladin's correspondence.\n\n\"If the indicator turns green, that doesn't certify drinkable water,\" she said. \"Run it at load, then test the output. Put the dates where everyone can see them.\"\n\nDanse examined the voltage specification. \"We should isolate the second circuit until the first passes.\"\n\n\"That would help,\" Aster said. \"Especially if you write it down.\"\n\nI watched him amend the log in the clipped, careful handwriting he used when a decision mattered.\n\nAfter she left, Danse closed the drawer with Haylen's letter inside. The sound was soft, not final.\n\n\"You don't have to tell me what she said,\" I reminded him.\n\n\"I know.\" He looked toward the silent purifier. \"It is difficult to distinguish withholding information from choosing not to surrender it.\"\n\n\"Maybe the difference is whose information it is.\"\n\nHe considered that without answering immediately. Finally he moved a stool beside the workbench for me.\n\nThe purifier remained untested. The sealed letter remained private. I sat with him through the quiet, not to make either problem vanish, but because he had asked me to stay.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI went inside.\n\nDanse stood beside the bench with a folded note in one hand. The packet of connectors lay unopened. His shoulders were set as if he expected an inspection.\n\n\"It's from Haylen,\" he said.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe handed me the paper.\n\nThe message was short. Haylen wrote that the Brotherhood had changed patrol routes near a settlement Danse once supplied. Some residents were afraid a former Paladin might try to use the settlement's old radio frequency. The letter did not ask Danse to return. It asked him to avoid the channel until the people who lived there had chosen a safer way to communicate.\n\nAt the end, in smaller writing, she'd added:\n\nI HOPE YOU ARE WELL. PLEASE DON'T ANSWER THROUGH THE OLD NET.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n\"She wants you safe,\" I said.\n\n\"She wants civilians safe.\"\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nDanse did not answer.\n\nHe took the note and placed it beside the purifier ledger.\n\n\"The old net is the most reliable frequency I know.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make it the right one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe sounded irritated.\n\n\"Danse.\"\n\n\"I know, Jasper.\"\n\nHe moved to the workbench and began sorting the connectors by size, though they were already sorted. The motion was familiar enough to make my chest ache.\n\nI waited until he stopped.\n\n\"What are you afraid of?\" I asked.\n\n\"That if I don't respond, she'll think I have forgotten her.\"\n\n\"And if you respond through the channel?\"\n\n\"People could be endangered.\"\n\n\"Then the answer isn't a radio message.\"\n\nHe looked at me, the conflict plain.\n\n\"She's one of the few people who knew me before everything changed.\"\n\nI stood beside him, close enough that our sleeves touched.\n\n\"You don't have to stop caring about her to respect the boundary she's giving you.\"\n\nHis breath caught.\n\n\"That's not what I meant.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I was trained to answer a call. Silence meant failure. Sometimes abandonment.\"\n\n\"Then maybe you need a different way to show you're still here.\"\n\nHe thought about it.\n\nThe following morning, we went to Neri with the note. Not to ask permission to answer Haylen, but to ask whether the Threadway courier network could deliver a sealed letter without identifying the settlement or exposing the person who had carried the first message.\n\nNeri listened and shook their head.\n\n\"Not without the recipient agreeing to receive it.\"\n\nDanse's jaw tightened.\n\n\"Then how do people communicate across the doors?\"\n\n\"They arrange it,\" Neri said. \"Or they wait.\"\n\n\"Waiting is unreliable.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it is the only honest answer.\"\n\nHe looked as though he wanted to argue. Instead, he asked for a blank page.\n\nWe returned to Brasswatch.\n\nHe wrote the letter slowly. I did not read over his shoulder. When he finished, he folded it and placed it in a plain envelope with no identifying marks on the outside.\n\nHe told me what he'd written only after he sealed it.\n\nHe had thanked Haylen for the warning. He had said he would not use the old network. He had told her that his life was different, and that the people who once served beside him still mattered. He had not asked her to put herself at risk for a reply.\n\n\"And now?\" I asked.\n\n\"Now I wait until there's a safe invitation.\"\n\nHe looked almost ill from the effort.\n\nI found two cups and poured water from the safe hand pump.\n\n\"That sounds difficult.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Welcome to the club.\"\n\nHe took the cup.\n\n\"You make light of everything.\"\n\n\"Not everything. Just the parts that might crush us if we never laugh.\"\n\nA small smile reached his mouth.\n\nBefore we returned to the purifier, Renn found Danse studying the sealed letter in his desk drawer.\n\n“Don't use the old courier seal,” they warned. “The mark on the outside is enough to tell somebody which network carried it.”\n\nDanse looked up sharply. “You recognized it?”\n\n“I recognized that you were hiding the mark under your hand. Different thing.”\n\nHe didn't enjoy the distinction. His first instinct was to ask exactly how much Renn knew and whether the letter needed to be confiscated. I saw his fingers close around the envelope.\n\nRenn had not asked to become an intelligence officer. They were carrying a worn connector and trying to keep the workbench safe.\n\n“The return label can be ordinary,” they said. “Use a permitted merchant address if the sender consents. No reason to put anyone else at risk because you want an answer today.”\n\nDanse set the letter down.\n\n“I am not asking you to handle it,” he said.\n\n“I wasn't offering.”\n\nAfter Renn left, he looked at the blank envelope for a long moment.\n\n“I used to think an unanswered message was a failure to maintain contact.”\n\n“Sometimes it's somebody choosing the safest way to live.”\n\nHe nodded. He put the original in a locked personal drawer, not the workshop records, and wrote a note reminding himself to ask before forwarding any part of Haylen's words.\n\nThat evening he repaired the purifier's cracked gasket instead of drafting another letter. The water ran clear through the test line; Renn recorded the measurement and signed it.\n\nThere was still a letter in the drawer. There was also a repaired machine. He had no need to make one stand in for the other.\n\n\"Are we getting a new parts supplier?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not yet,\" he said.\n\nRenn held up the connector packet Haylen had sent. \"These might fit the old regulator. I wasn't going to open them without asking.\"\n\nDanse thanked them. They compared the connectors with the workshop's inventory, noting which ones could be safely tested and which would need a separate circuit check. He didn't use the parts merely because they had come from someone he trusted. A good source could still send the wrong component.\n\n\"Should we credit the sender in the public repairs ledger?\" Renn asked.\n\nDanse's hand paused.\n\n\"No personal name,\" he said. \"Record the specification and the inspection result. If the supplier ever wants to be publicly identified, that decision should be theirs.\"\n\nRenn nodded and wrote *private donation, pending test* instead. I saw a little relief move through Danse's shoulders.\n\nHe wasn't closing off Haylen's part in his life. He was keeping a correspondence safe until she could choose another way to receive it.\n\n\"You can miss somebody without transmitting on an unsafe channel,\" I said when Renn left.\n\n\"I am discovering that silence requires substantially more discipline than I was taught.\"\n\n\"Well, there's something they don't put on the recruitment poster.\"\n\nHe gave me a long look, then returned to the actual repair instructions. The connector remained on the bench until it could be tested. The letter stayed sealed.\n\nWe worked the rest of the day on the purifier's intake pipe. Severus had warned us about residues that appeared after the system sat idle. Danse had taken the criticism seriously enough to redesign the testing schedule. Every sample would be collected and labeled before anyone declared the water safe.\n\nAt noon, Tamsin stopped by with a list of residents who needed deliveries until the repairs were complete. Danse read the list carefully.\n\n\"Three households require assistance carrying water,\" he said.\n\n\"Four,\" I corrected. \"One person doesn't want their name announced publicly.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"Then we can arrange a route without displaying it.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"You're learning.\"\n\n\"I was already capable of discretion.\"\n\n\"Understatement of the century.\"\n\nHe shook his head, but there was warmth in it.\n\nThe letter stayed in a locked drawer. Not because he had stopped wanting to send it, but because wanting didn't give him the right to use a channel that might harm someone else.\n\nThat evening he found me on the workshop steps.\n\n\"Could I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe sat beside me, the old scarf loose around his neck.\n\n\"Do you think caring about someone far away makes what I have with you less important?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"No. You know what I believe about loving more than one person. But even outside romance, care isn't a resource we use up by remembering people.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"I still have trouble believing that.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I should say something I do believe.\" He looked directly at me. \"I want you here tonight. Not because Haylen isn't here. Because you are.\"\n\nWarmth settled behind my ribs.\n\n\"That I can do.\"\n\nHe touched my cheek with one hand, waiting for the small answering movement that told him I wanted the kiss. I leaned into his palm.\n\nHis mouth was warm. His beard scratched lightly against my skin. He was still learning how to be gentle without making gentleness feel like a protocol.\n\nWhen we parted, he kept his forehead against mine for a moment.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said.\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not asking me to forget.\"\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe next morning, the letter was still in the drawer.\n\nThe purifier was still offline.\n\nBoth were unfinished.\n\nFor the first time, Danse seemed willing to let unfinished mean that he could keep caring without taking the wrong action simply to make the waiting stop.","word_count":1954},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Night the Lights Went Out","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The generator failed with a sound like a very large person clearing their throat.\n\nThen the entire eastern side of Lanternwell Square went dark.\n\nI was at Brasswatch Yard with Danse, checking the volunteer repair schedule. One moment the workshop lights were humming; the next they were gone. Outside, somebody shouted. A few seconds later the emergency lantern by the door came on.\n\nDanse was already moving.\n\n\"Stay back from the panel,\" he said.\n\n\"I wasn't planning to touch it.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe opened the insulated access cover and looked at the gauges. The smell of overheated wiring hung in the air, sharp and unpleasant. The generator had been carrying extra load since the storm flooded the lower service route. We knew it needed inspection. We hadn't expected it to fail tonight.\n\nA runner arrived from the square.\n\n\"The hall's dark. People are sheltering from the weather.\"\n\nDanse looked at the panel.\n\n\"I can restore power.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"Not until I know what failed.\"\n\nThat was the right answer, though it hurt to hear it.\n\nWe brought the portable lanterns to the hall. Maui was already carrying blankets to the west benches. Katara was checking that the children had their caregivers nearby. Neri had a paper list of people who had chosen to wait there, with no public record of private destinations.\n\nI helped set lanterns where they wouldn't block the exit.\n\nDanse spoke to the volunteers.\n\n\"We need a trained person at the generator, two people to watch the backup lamps, and someone to check the cables for water damage. No one enters the electrical room without protective equipment.\"\n\nHe didn't assign people by pointing at them. He asked who was trained and available.\n\nRenn raised a hand.\n\n\"I can check the cable run. I trained on that system last week.\"\n\nDanse looked at them.\n\n\"With me.\"\n\nAster volunteered for the backup lamps. Pip offered to maintain the clear path, then asked to be relieved after an hour.\n\nEverything moved more slowly than a military emergency would have.\n\nIt also moved with people who knew what they'd agreed to do.\n\nWe found the fault near the old canal service junction. Water had reached an improperly sealed conduit. One breaker had tripped, and the generator's load control had tried to compensate until it shut down.\n\nDanse stared at the panel.\n\n\"I approved that conduit inspection last month.\"\n\n\"Was it completed?\" I asked.\n\nHe checked the log.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nI felt a cold knot in my stomach.\n\n\"Then we document that.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThere would be time later for accountability. Right now we needed safe lights, dry equipment, and a plan that didn't make the next repair more dangerous than the outage itself.\n\nRenn tested the replacement cable while Danse checked the voltage. Aster reported that two backup lamps were losing power. We moved the remaining lights to the highest-traffic routes and made sure people knew which corridors were still dark.\n\nNo one was ordered to stay in the hall.\n\nNo one was forced through a Threadway.\n\nNear midnight, a deep note sounded from the direction of Tideglass. The second bell.\n\nDanse looked toward the door.\n\n\"Pressure in the lower route,\" he said.\n\n\"Could it affect the generator?\"\n\n\"Possibly. We should assume the service junction is still wet until we test it.\"\n\nAster handed him a folded delivery sheet. Beren had drawn a thick line around the north approach and crossed out the service corridor. Edda's initials appeared beside the turn that needed extra clearance.\n\n“Water for the east shelter,” Aster said. “Amel says they need the first load tonight. The cart's coming around the long way, but it still has to pass this door.”\n\nDanse studied the map. The drawing was rough, more useful than any elegant plan I'd seen in the workshop office. Someone had marked the narrow step with a black square. The crossing was too tight for a cart if we stacked the spare cable reels beside it.\n\n“Those reels have to move,” he said.\n\nRenn looked over his shoulder. “Not into the electrical room.”\n\n“Agreed. We can put them in the dry store—after checking the exit remains clear.”\n\nI watched him start to divide the job, then stop.\n\n“Aster, do you have the clearance measurements?”\n\n“Edda checked them with Beren. I've got the note.”\n\n“Good. Show us the dry store and tell me whether a cart can turn there.”\n\nIt was not the command voice he'd once used when the answer was already written in his head. He still sounded like a soldier, and he still made every sentence as precise as he could. But he was waiting for a volunteer's measurements before moving anything.\n\nOutside, the square remained dark. I could hear water sloshing in the cart as it approached the corner. The sound did something to me that the generator's silence hadn't: it reminded me that the numbers Danse was looking at belonged to people who were thirsty.\n\n“Will you be at the panel when the cart passes?” I asked.\n\n“No. Renn can watch the indicator. I'll help keep the route clear.”\n\n“You're sure?”\n\nHe glanced at the drawing. “The man delivering water has already changed his route for us. We can change one doorway for him.”\n\nHe folded the note carefully and handed it back. The power could wait for a safe test. The water could not wait for his pride.\n\nHe refused to restart the system immediately.\n\nA few people complained.\n\nOne man shouted that the old Brotherhood would have had the lights back already.\n\nDanse looked at him.\n\n\"The old Brotherhood might have ordered someone into a flooded electrical room. That doesn't make it safe.\"\n\nThe man stopped.\n\nI watched Danse turn back to the panel.\n\nIt was a small victory, but one he'd earned with work rather than a speech.\n\nAt two in the morning Aster returned from the shelter carrying the last charged emergency lamp.\n\n\"One of the halls is too dark,\" they said. \"The lanterns are bright enough near the main door, but the side passage doesn't have any light.\"\n\nDanse checked the diagram.\n\n\"We could move one from the equipment room.\"\n\nRenn shook their head. \"We need that light to see the shutdown indicator if the backup system fails.\"\n\nI looked between them.\n\n\"Then what's the other option?\"\n\nNobody answered immediately.\n\nRenn brought the load sheet over from the generator panel. In the light of the emergency lantern, the numbers looked like little black insects crawling down the page.\n\n\"We can keep the clinic refrigerator on backup,\" they said. \"Not the repair presses. Not tonight.\"\n\nDanse took the sheet. \"The press line could be shut down by section.\"\n\n\"It already is,\" Renn answered. \"You don't need to go back and switch it off a second time.\"\n\nI watched his expression. He hated being told he was too late to solve something, even when the problem had been solved well.\n\nAster came in from the hall with a damp coat and a question about the east exit. Someone had moved a spare crate to make space for a lantern, and now the route was too narrow for the cart carrying drinking water.\n\nDanse turned toward the door.\n\nRenn stopped him. \"I'll take the panel. You can check the hall with Aster. One problem at a time.\"\n\nHe hesitated, then handed back the sheet.\n\nI followed him into the corridor. The floor felt colder without the workshop lights. In the dark a small mistake seemed enormous; people were whispering, and someone had dropped a spoon that rang across the tiles like a bell.\n\n\"We've trained for power loss,\" Danse said under his breath.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why do I feel as though I'm improvising?\"\n\n\"Because this is happening to real people, not a training map.\"\n\nHe stared at the crate and helped Aster move it clear. The work was ordinary and unglamorous. He could have thrown the box aside without breaking a sweat, but he waited while Aster checked where it belonged. Renn was still monitoring the generator. Nobody needed him to command both places at once.\n\n\"Is this enough?\" he asked me.\n\n\"To make the path clear? Let's ask the person who uses it.\"\n\nWe did. The cart passed without catching the wall. Aster wrote the clearance in the log, and Danse looked less interested in being the first to claim the crisis was over.\n\nWhen he returned to the panel, Renn had new readings. He bent over the figures and listened.\n\nPip arrived with a sketch of the hall showing the two routes people were actually using. One led to the kitchens; the other went to the sheltered waiting rooms. The lamp beside the closed storage door had been placed there because the old plan showed it as an entrance.\n\n\"Could we move that one?\" I asked.\n\nPip nodded. \"The storage door's locked and signed. Nobody needs to go through it tonight.\"\n\nDanse considered.\n\n\"Provided the exit markings remain visible.\"\n\nThey walked the route together before moving anything. Aster carried the lamp, Renn checked the cable was clear of the footpath, and Pip tested whether the light could be seen from the hall's far corner.\n\nAn older traveler asked whether they could still leave by the eastern route.\n\n\"Yes,\" Pip said. \"It is open if you choose to use it. We can show you the map.\"\n\nThe traveler chose to wait until morning instead.\n\nNo one argued.\n\nBack at the yard, Danse wrote the temporary arrangement on the board and assigned a recheck before dawn.\n\n\"This system needs a permanent backup,\" he said.\n\n\"With a budget and a maintenance schedule,\" I added.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\nI took his hand briefly.\n\nHe was still learning the hardest part: a good emergency plan did not depend on him staying awake forever. It depended on other people being able to follow it when he went home.\n\nThe backup power held until dawn. When the water level fell, the team completed the insulation test, replaced the damaged seal, and restarted the generator at reduced load. Lights returned to the workshop first, then the square.\n\nThere was no applause.\n\nThere was a list of repairs.\n\nWe posted the temporary load limit, the inspection results, and the next scheduled test. Renn added their signature beside the work they'd actually performed. Danse added his to the line documenting the missed conduit inspection.\n\nBy the time the last lantern was collected, my hands were shaking with exhaustion.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\n\"You're cold.\"\n\n\"I'm tired.\"\n\n\"Then come home.\"\n\n\"There's still work.\"\n\n\"There's always work.\"\n\nHe said it with such quiet conviction that I looked up.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"Renn and Aster have the next shift. Tamsin will review the repair. I've left the equipment in a safe state.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked to his room above the workshop. The familiar smell of clean metal and dry wood met me as he opened the door. He set the toolbelt aside, took off his coat, and stood for a moment looking at the lamp on the table.\n\n\"I used to feel better only when the lights came back,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I feel better knowing nobody was hurt because I wanted to restore them too quickly.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"That matters.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you want to stay?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because you want to, not because it's late?\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nHe laughed softly.\n\nHis kiss was careful at first, then warmer when I rested my hands against his chest. We stood close enough to hear the rain easing outside. No one needed a heroic performance from us.\n\nWhen he asked if I wanted to go inside, I said yes.\n\nWe closed the door and left the rest of the night private.\n\nIn the morning, there was a note from Renn.\n\nThe generator had passed its second test.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller writing, they had added: THANK YOU FOR NOT RUSHING.\n\nDanse read it twice.\n\nThen he folded it and put it in his pocket.","word_count":2003},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-05":{"number":5,"title":"Righteous Authority","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 5 of 40","content":"I spent the next morning taking apart Paladin Danse’s rifle.\n\nTechnically it was my rifle now.\n\nEmotionally, it still felt like handling someone else’s coat.\n\nRighteous Authority lay in pieces across the workbench at Cambridge Police Station: focusing lens, capacitor, barrel assembly, stock, screws lined in a row because I was not an animal. The modifications were careful and practical. Nothing decorative. Nothing added unless it improved function.\n\nIt looked exactly like something Danse would build.\n\nHe appeared beside me halfway through reassembly.\n\nI did not hear him approach because, for once, he was out of power armor.\n\nThat was disorienting enough that I nearly dropped a spring.\n\nWithout the steel shell he was still broad, still carried himself with military rigidity, but he looked more human in the simple Brotherhood uniform. Tired, too. There were shadows beneath his eyes I had not been able to see behind the helmet.\n\n“You’re changing the focusing array,” he said.\n\n“Good morning to you too.”\n\n“That array is calibrated.”\n\n“For your grip.”\n\n“It’s adjustable.”\n\n“So am I.”\n\nHe leaned over the bench.\n\nHis shoulder came close enough to mine that I smelled soap, gun oil, and the metallic scent that clung to people who spent too much time inside powered armor.\n\nI forgot what I was doing for one entire second.\n\nDanse noticed the part in my hand.\n\n“You installed that backward.”\n\nI looked down.\n\nI had.\n\n“Testing you.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\nHe reached past me and corrected it.\n\nHis forearm brushed mine.\n\nThe contact was ordinary.\n\nMy reaction to it was stupid.\n\nI stepped sideways under the pretense of reaching for a tool.\n\nDanse continued as if nothing had happened.\n\n“Your modification is sound, but shorten the stock by half an inch. You’re compensating for the length every time you shoulder it.”\n\n“You watched me that closely?”\n\n“I watch everyone under my command.”\n\n“I’m not under your command.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\n“Not officially.”\n\nThat stopped me.\n\nHe looked irritated with himself.\n\nThen he straightened.\n\n“I want to offer you a position.”\n\nI turned fully toward him.\n\n“What kind?”\n\n“The Brotherhood is always looking for capable people. You’ve already demonstrated that you can handle yourself in combat and that you understand technology.”\n\n“Understand is generous.”\n\n“You took apart a custom laser rifle after one night of owning it.”\n\n“Curiosity.”\n\n“Technical curiosity is useful.”\n\n“So you’re recruiting me.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Is this the speech where you tell me I’ll be saving humanity?”\n\n“If you’d like.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe did not.\n\nThen, unexpectedly, he sat on the edge of the opposite workbench.\n\n“I’m not going to pretend the Brotherhood is perfect.”\n\nThat was not the sentence I expected.\n\nDanse looked toward the front of the station where Haylen was working the transmitter.\n\n“But it gave me something I didn’t have before. Structure. Training. People I could depend on. A reason to believe surviving could mean more than getting through another day.”\n\nThe humor left me.\n\n“That’s what purpose means to you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nNot slogans.\n\nNot doctrine.\n\nA place where an orphaned scavenger had been told he belonged.\n\nI looked back down at the weapon.\n\n“Rhys hates me.”\n\n“Rhys distrusts anyone who isn’t Brotherhood.”\n\n“That is a generous interpretation of his personality.”\n\nDanse’s mouth twitched.\n\n“He’s a good soldier.”\n\n“I didn’t say he wasn’t.”\n\n“Haylen likes you.”\n\n“Haylen has taste.”\n\nThis time the smile happened.\n\nBrief, but undeniable.\n\n“I’m undecided,” he said.\n\n“Cruel.”\n\n“I said what I said.”\n\nI finished the rifle and lifted it to my shoulder.\n\nThe shorter stock fit better.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n“Better.”\n\n“You were right.”\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\n“Do you practice being unbearable?”\n\n“No practice necessary.”\n\nI stared at him.\n\nHe realized what he had said a heartbeat later.\n\nHaylen, who had absolutely been listening from the next room, choked on a laugh.\n\nDanse looked offended by his own joke.\n\nI liked him instantly more for it.\n\nThe transmitter crackled that afternoon.\n\nNot a clean message. Barely words. But a response.\n\nHaylen sat upright so quickly her chair rolled backward.\n\n“Paladin!”\n\nDanse crossed the room in three strides.\n\nStatic filled the station.\n\nThen a voice emerged, distant and broken.\n\nBrotherhood call signs. Coordinates. Confirmation.\n\nHome, as far as Danse was concerned, speaking through the noise.\n\nI watched his face change.\n\nRelief first.\n\nThen discipline locked back into place.\n\nHe began issuing instructions immediately. Record the transmission. Confirm power levels. Prepare a full report. Inventory supplies. Check ammunition.\n\nHe was happiest when there was something to do with a feeling.\n\nThat was useful information.\n\nThe operator kept one finger on the tuning dial while Haylen copied the call signs onto an empty requisition sheet. A second burst of static came through; Rhys lunged for the volume and nearly drowned out the last coordinate.\n\n\"Leave it,\" Danse said, sharp enough to stop him.\n\nRhys froze. Danse took the receiver himself, counted a silent beat, and eased the dial back until the voice returned.\n\n\"Correction,\" he said after a moment. \"Haylen, you have the clearer ear. Confirm the final group.\"\n\nShe repeated the numbers. He entered them on the report exactly as she gave them, then marked the unconfirmed portion with a question instead of filling it from memory.\n\nI had heard him give dozens of orders. I had not expected the little pause before he asked for her judgment. It changed my impression of him more than another successful shot would have.\n\nWhen Haylen handed me the spare pencil, our fingers were blackened from the same cheap carbon paper. Danse noticed and placed a rag within reach without comment.\n\n\"Was that your idea of being considerate?\" I asked, too quietly for the others.\n\n\"Leaving graphite on the weapon components would be poor maintenance.\"\n\n\"Ah. Purely tactical kindness.\"\n\nHis ears reddened, just slightly. He reviewed the coordinates again rather than answer. I kept the rag anyway.\n\nOutside, wind rattled the old police-station windows. The Brotherhood voice went quiet, but the report stayed honest: two confirmed numbers, one gap. For a place held together by salvaged metal and confidence, that seemed worth more than pretending we had heard everything.\n\nLater, when the station settled and Rhys had finally stopped complaining about his leg, I found Danse alone in the garage inspecting the damaged plates of his power armor.\n\n“You going to sleep?” I asked.\n\n“Eventually.”\n\n“That means no.”\n\n“I have maintenance to finish.”\n\n“You almost got cooked alive yesterday.”\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\n“You fought ghouls before that.”\n\n“I remember.”\n\n“You also walked several miles in a metal suit that weighs more than a brahmin.”\n\n“Your point?”\n\n“My point is that exhaustion is not a moral virtue.”\n\nDanse stopped tightening a bolt.\n\nFor a second I thought I had crossed the line again.\n\nThen he set the wrench down.\n\n“You always this persistent?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I was afraid of that.”\n\nI leaned against the workbench.\n\n“Go sleep. I can finish checking the seals.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe looked at me sharply.\n\nIt was the first time I had used his name without the title.\n\nThe garage went strangely quiet.\n\nI had not meant anything by it.\n\nOr I had meant too many things by it and none of them were safe to inspect.\n\nHis eyes stayed on mine.\n\nThen he looked down at the wrench.\n\n“Fine,” he said.\n\nI blinked.\n\n“That worked?”\n\n“Don’t get used to it.”\n\nHe stood.\n\nAt the doorway he paused.\n\n“The left shoulder actuator sticks under heavy load.”\n\n“I noticed.”\n\n“The replacement coupling is in the red toolbox.”\n\n“Go to bed.”\n\nHis expression softened almost invisibly.\n\n“Yes, ma’am,” he started, then stopped before committing to a gendered honorific, scowled at the linguistic problem, and settled on, “Civilian.”\n\nI grinned.\n\n“Good night, Paladin.”\n\nHe left shaking his head.\n\nI turned back to the power armor.\n\nThere are intimate things in the Commonwealth that have nothing to do with skin.\n\nKnowing which joint in a man’s armor catches when he is tired, I would learn, was one of them.\n\nLater that afternoon, he caught me cleaning carbon scoring from the forearm plate.\n\n“You missed lunch,” he said.\n\n“So did you.”\n\n“I was conducting an inventory.”\n\n“I was conducting maintenance.”\n\n“That is not the same thing.”\n\n“It is if we both forgot to eat.”\n\nDanse looked toward the station’s improvised kitchen, then back at the armor.\n\n“I did not forget.”\n\n“You strategically postponed chewing?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe disappeared into the back room and returned with two dented tins and a packet of crackers old enough to qualify as archaeological evidence.\n\nHe placed one ration beside me.\n\n“What’s this?”\n\n“Food.”\n\n“I recognize the broad category.”\n\n“You criticized my meal schedule.”\n\n“So you brought me yours?”\n\n“I brought you a separate ration.”\n\n“Which you definitely planned to eat before I pointed out you hadn’t.”\n\nDanse sat on the edge of the desk and opened his tin with unnecessary concentration.\n\n“I’m reconsidering the wisdom of rewarding your observations.”\n\nWe ate in companionable silence for almost a minute.\n\nThen I asked, “Why that rifle?”\n\nHis eyes dropped to Righteous Authority where it rested across the bench.\n\n“It’s reliable.”\n\n“That’s not why you looked like you were handing me a medal when you gave it to me.”\n\nDanse stopped chewing.\n\n“I did not look like that.”\n\n“You did a little.”\n\nHe set the ration down.\n\n“I gave it to you because you earned it.”\n\nThe words came stiffly, as if he disliked having to explain praise once it had already escaped.\n\n“You entered a Brotherhood operation with no obligation to us. You followed orders under fire, adapted when plans changed, and did not abandon me when the engine room became… complicated.”\n\n“Complicated is one word for it.”\n\n“I had expected you to retreat.”\n\n“Were you disappointed when I didn’t?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis gaze stayed on mine.\n\n“Quite the opposite.”\n\nHeat climbed somewhere beneath my collar. I focused on the rifle because the rifle was safe.\n\n“So it’s not a recruitment bribe?”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Not even a little?”\n\n“The Brotherhood could use someone with your abilities.”\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“That does not make the weapon a bribe.”\n\n“Recruitment gift.”\n\n“Recognition of service.”\n\n“Very romantic phrasing.”\n\nDanse nearly inhaled a cracker.\n\nThe coughing fit lasted long enough for Haylen to look over from the transmitter.\n\n“Everything all right, Paladin?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis answer came much too quickly.\n\nI stared very seriously at the rifle.\n\nHaylen’s eyes moved from Danse to me and back again.\n\nHer smile was terrible.\n\n“I’m sure.”\n\nShe returned to her work.\n\nDanse lowered his voice.\n\n“You did that intentionally.”\n\n“I have no idea what you mean.”\n\n“Outstanding.”\n\nI bit down on my grin.\n\nHe finished his ration, but his ears stayed red for the rest of the meal.\n\nThat was the day I learned the Brotherhood’s power armor had less defensive value against flirting than advertised.\n","word_count":1792},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Example","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 27 of 40","content":"Maxson looked at Danse and saw an enemy.\n\nDanse looked at Maxson and saw home.\n\nThat was why the confrontation hurt to watch.\n\nThe elder entered Listening Post Bravo alone, though I had no doubt soldiers waited above. His coat was damp from the rain. His hand rested near his weapon.\n\nDanse immediately came to attention.\n\nReflex.\n\nYears of training stronger than humiliation.\n\n“Elder.”\n\nMaxson’s face hardened.\n\n“Don’t call me that.”\n\nDanse flinched.\n\nI stepped closer to him.\n\nMaxson noticed.\n\n“So this is how it is.”\n\n“This is how what is?” I asked.\n\n“You were ordered to execute a synth infiltrator.”\n\n“I found Danse.”\n\n“You found a machine wearing his face.”\n\nDanse went rigid.\n\nI felt anger rise.\n\nBefore I could speak, Danse did.\n\n“He’s right.”\n\nI turned on him.\n\n“No.”\n\n“Stay out of this.”\n\n“Absolutely not.”\n\nMaxson’s gaze moved between us.\n\n“Paladin Danse is dead. He was never real.”\n\nSomething in Danse’s face collapsed.\n\nI stepped forward.\n\n“He was real enough to command your recon team.”\n\n“Because the Institute designed him to deceive.”\n\n“Then they did a terrible job, because he has spent years trying to destroy them.”\n\n“You don’t know his purpose.”\n\n“Neither do you.”\n\nMaxson’s eyes sharpened.\n\n“I know what he is.”\n\nDanse’s voice came low.\n\n“Elder, after everything I’ve done—”\n\nMaxson rounded on him.\n\n“You expect your service to excuse your nature?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer was immediate.\n\nDanse straightened.\n\n“If my death protects the Brotherhood, I accept it.”\n\nI felt physically sick.\n\nMaxson looked almost relieved.\n\nOf course Danse would obey.\n\nThat was the tragedy.\n\n“I don’t,” I said.\n\nBoth men looked at me.\n\nDanse’s expression became furious.\n\n“This isn’t your decision.”\n\n“Yes, it is. You told me to do it.”\n\n“You have your orders.”\n\n“And I refuse them.”\n\nMaxson stepped closer.\n\n“Then you place yourself against the Brotherhood.”\n\n“No. I place one person above one bad order.”\n\n“That is how organizations rot.”\n\n“Then maybe organizations should survive questions.”\n\nDanse whispered my name designation in warning.\n\nI ignored him.\n\n“Look at him,” I told Maxson.\n\n“I am.”\n\n“No. You’re looking at a category. Look at him.”\n\nMaxson’s jaw tightened.\n\n“Danse would have killed a synth under these circumstances.”\n\nThe statement hit all three of us.\n\nBecause it was true.\n\nDanse closed his eyes.\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “And maybe finding out what that feels like is part of why he deserves the chance to become better.”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\nPain, anger, understanding.\n\nMaxson said nothing.\n\nI continued.\n\n“If the Institute wanted an infiltrator, why give him a life that made him loyal to you? Why let him bleed beside your soldiers? Why let him lose people and grieve them? Why build someone capable of choosing death for your ideals?”\n\n“Programming.”\n\n“Then humans are just training and chemistry with better marketing.”\n\nMaxson’s hand moved toward his weapon.\n\nDanse stepped between us.\n\n“Enough.”\n\nHe faced Maxson.\n\n“I won’t allow bloodshed over me.”\n\n“You don’t get to order me anymore,” I said.\n\nDanse’s mouth tightened.\n\n“Habit.”\n\nEven then.\n\nEven there.\n\nThe stupid callback nearly broke me.\n\nMaxson stared at Danse for a long time.\n\nThen his hand moved away from the weapon.\n\n“You cannot return.”\n\nDanse did not move.\n\n“You are stripped of rank, authority, and Brotherhood protection. If you approach our installations, you will be treated as hostile.”\n\nEach sentence removed a piece of him.\n\nDanse stood through all of them.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nMaxson’s expression flickered.\n\nMaybe regret.\n\nMaybe anger.\n\nMaybe nothing I had the right to name.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“You are responsible for him.”\n\nI almost laughed at the irony.\n\n“I have been for a while.”\n\nDanse shot me a look.\n\nMaxson turned and left.\n\nThe elevator doors closed above us.\n\nSilence filled the bunker.\n\nDanse remained at attention.\n\n“Danse.”\n\nNothing.\n\nI touched his shoulder.\n\nHis knees gave out.\n\nI caught him badly.\n\nWe ended up on the floor together, Danse half against me, one hand gripping my sleeve.\n\nHe did not cry this time.\n\nThat would have been easier.\n\nHe stared at the wall.\n\n“It’s gone.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Everything.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“My rank. My unit. My home.”\n\n“Not everything.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI knew what he wanted me to say.\n\nMe.\n\nUs.\n\nLove.\n\nI refused to turn myself into a consolation prize.\n\n“You still have yourself,” I said.\n\nDanse laughed once, without humor.\n\n“That’s the part I’m least certain exists.”\n\nI held his face between my hands.\n\n“Then we find out.”\n\nHe closed his eyes.\n\n“What if I don’t like the answer?”\n\n“Then you keep looking.”\n\nHis hands covered mine.\n\nFor the first time since I met him, Paladin Danse had no mission.\n\nOnly a life.\n\nIt frightened him more.\n\nMaxson’s departure left a sound behind.\n\nNot literal footsteps.\n\nAuthority.\n\nFor hours afterward Danse kept turning toward the elevator as if another order might descend and tell him what came next.\n\nNone did.\n\nI heated water because making tea felt less offensive than making his coffee.\n\nHe sat at the table with his holotags in front of him.\n\nThe Brotherhood emblem caught the bunker light.\n\n“Do you want those?” I asked.\n\nHis fingers closed around the metal.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI had expected hesitation.\n\n“Even now?”\n\n“Especially now.”\n\nHe looked at the tag.\n\n“This was mine.”\n\nThe statement was simple and fierce.\n\n“Whatever else is false, I wore this. I earned the rank attached to it. The missions happened.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHis gaze sharpened.\n\n“Good?”\n\n“You sounded like you for a second.”\n\nHe looked down again.\n\n“I don’t know what that means anymore.”\n\n“Neither do I. Keep going.”\n\nThe ventilation fan clicked above us. Danse's head turned automatically toward the noise. His body still expected the bunker to ask something of him.\n\n\"You don't have to inspect that,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe glanced at the ceiling once more. \"I used to find it reassuring. A fault I could identify. A part I could replace.\"\n\n\"Now?\"\n\n\"Now I don't know which things are faults and which things are simply me.\"\n\nI wanted to promise him there was a neat distinction. I also knew better than to turn a person into a machine because that was the language he had been taught to trust.\n\n\"Do you want me to stay while you talk?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you want me to do anything?\"\n\nHe studied the tag between his fingers. \"Don't take that away. Not yet.\"\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\n\"I know.\" His mouth tightened. \"It is difficult to believe I still get to make decisions about it.\"\n\nI shifted until the edge of the metal no longer dug into his palm. He let me, then took it back when he was ready. That was a small, stubborn decision, and I was glad I had not made it for him.\n\nHe stared toward the wall, breathing unevenly. I stayed beside him without inventing an assignment to fill the silence. The next memory arrived because he chose to tell it, not because I had demanded the correct answer to his grief.\n\nDanse turned the tag over between thumb and forefinger.\n\n“I remember my first day at the Citadel.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\n“I remember being terrified I would fail inspection.”\n\n“Very on-brand.”\n\nA faint exhale.\n\n“I remember Cutler hiding one of my boots.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“I remember earning my first promotion. I remember the pride.”\n\nHis voice shook.\n\n“If those memories belonged to someone else first, does that make the pride theft?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You answered quickly.”\n\n“Because whoever supplied the memory didn’t stand through your inspection. You did.”\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n“The memory may have put you in the room. The choice to keep showing up afterward was yours.”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“You keep separating origin from action.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Convenient.”\n\n“For you? Extremely.”\n\nThat earned the ghost of a smile.\n\nWe drank the tea.\n\nIt was terrible in a different way than his coffee.\n\nNeither of us complained.\n\nLater, while I checked the bunker’s upper entrance, Danse followed me with his rifle.\n\n“You don’t have to patrol,” I said.\n\n“We need security.”\n\n“We also need you to sleep.”\n\n“I won’t.”\n\n“Then lie down and fail at it horizontally.”\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\nI kept inspecting the door.\n\nAt the top of the stairs, cold night air drifted through the ruins. No vertibird lights crossed the sky.\n\nDanse stood beside me without armor, suddenly vulnerable in a way I hated.\n\n“Maxson spared me because of you,” he said.\n\n“Maxson chose not to kill you.”\n\n“Because you forced the issue.”\n\n“Don’t make my argument erase his decision.”\n\nDanse was silent.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“You told me once command means living with consequences. So does mercy.”\n\n“He may regret it.”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“I may prove him wrong.”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“You have got to stop saying that.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nWind moved across the broken concrete.\n\n“Do you regret it?” he asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Refusing the order.”\n\nI thought about the Prydwen, Haylen, Rhys, the possibility that the Brotherhood would never welcome me again. I thought about the weapon I had carried into the bunker in case Maxson’s fear proved true.\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse searched my face.\n\n“Not even a little?”\n\n“I’m scared. That’s different.”\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n“I’m scared too.”\n\nThat was new.\n\nNot *concerned*. Not *assessing risk.*\n\nScared.\n\nI leaned against him.\n\nHe hesitated, then put an arm around me.\n\nNo rank.\n\nNo orders.\n\nTwo frightened people in a ruined building, trying not to confuse certainty with courage.\n\nWe stayed outside longer than either of us needed to.\n\nDanse watched the road Maxson had taken.\n\n“Do you think he meant it?” he asked.\n\n“What?”\n\n“That I should stay out of the Brotherhood’s sight.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“And if they see me?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nHe nodded as if uncertainty were an order he could obey.\n\nThen he looked down at his empty chest where the Brotherhood insignia would normally have been.\n\n“I spent years believing exile was something that happened to people who failed.”\n\n“You didn’t fail.”\n\n“Maxson would disagree.”\n\n“Maxson is allowed to be wrong.”\n\nThat earned me a faint, disbelieving laugh.\n\n“You say that very easily.”\n\n“I’ve had practice.”\n\nDanse looked toward the bunker door.\n\n“I don’t know what I am supposed to be now.”\n\n“Cold, apparently.”\n\nHe blinked.\n\nI bumped my shoulder against his.\n\n“One problem at a time.”\n\nThe first step toward the bunker made him stop.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“My tags.”\n\nHe touched the place where Brotherhood holotags usually rested beneath his uniform.\n\n“They took them?”\n\n“No. I left them inside.”\n\n“Want them?”\n\nHe thought.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nNot because Maxson recognized the service anymore. Because Danse did.\n\nThat distinction seemed important enough not to say aloud.\n\nAfter a while Danse said, “We should go inside. Temperature is dropping.”\n\n“Romantic.”\n\n“Hypothermia is not.”\n\n“See? Still you.”\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\nThis time the smile stayed.\n","word_count":1796},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-45":{"number":45,"title":"When the Plan Changes","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"Danse had set out two mugs before I told him I couldn't stay.\n\nHe didn't say anything at first. He looked at the table, then at my face, then at the clock above the workshop door.\n\n\"I thought we had agreed on dinner.\"\n\n\"We did.\"\n\n\"You've made another commitment?\"\n\n\"Yes. Greg needs help arranging supplies for a music night at Starwake. I told him I'd come over after the afternoon repairs.\"\n\nDanse nodded once. His expression went blank in the way it did when he was trying to turn an emotional response into a procedure.\n\n\"I see.\"\n\nI took off my coat, then put it back on. The movement felt ridiculous.\n\n\"I should have checked our schedule before saying yes,\" I admitted. \"I'm sorry.\"\n\n\"Is this an emergency?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Then why must our plans change?\"\n\nThe question had an edge. Not an accusation, exactly. A real question from someone who had spent years living by orders and wasn't always certain how to ask for something without sounding as though he was issuing one.\n\n\"I don't know that they must,\" I said. \"I want to keep both promises. I haven't worked out how.\"\n\nHe exhaled through his nose.\n\n\"Then we should work it out.\"\n\nWe stood there looking at the table.\n\nIt was such a small thing, two mugs and a meal we'd planned to share, but I knew how much the evening meant to him. Danse didn't need an elaborate dinner. He wanted the predictability of a time I'd chosen to spend with him, not a gap left over after every other obligation had been met.\n\n\"Can we sit?\" I asked.\n\nHe pulled out a chair.\n\nI did the same.\n\nFor a moment we sat in silence, listening to the ticking clock and the faint hum of the workshop furnace.\n\n\"I don't want you to feel as though you have to compete,\" I said.\n\nDanse's shoulders tightened.\n\n\"I haven't said that.\"\n\n\"No. But I can see something is bothering you.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I was under the impression that our arrangements were clear.\"\n\n\"They are. I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. You know I care about you, and that I also care about other people. But that doesn't excuse me for forgetting a plan.\"\n\nHe rubbed one thumb against the edge of the mug.\n\n\"I don't want to be the person who demands you cancel everything else.\"\n\n\"You're not.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I don't know where the line is.\"\n\n\"Between asking for reassurance and making a demand?\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI leaned forward.\n\n\"Tell me what you actually need.\"\n\nDanse stared at the clock again, as though it might provide a regulation for this situation.\n\n\"I wanted a quiet evening,\" he said finally. \"Not because I wanted to prevent you from seeing anyone else. Because I wanted to be with you without worrying that something would pull us apart in the middle of it.\"\n\nI felt the familiar sting of realizing I'd hurt someone by treating their time as flexible simply because they were patient.\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then what do we do?\"\n\nI took out my notebook and checked the time. The supply meeting at Starwake was supposed to be a short one. Greg had asked for help with chairs and a sound check, but Mara and Pip were both already scheduled for the first hour. There was no reason I had to stay through the evening.\n\n\"I can go to the setup meeting, then come back for dinner at seven,\" I said. \"If you're willing to eat later.\"\n\nDanse considered that.\n\n\"I can.\"\n\n\"And if something changes, I'll send word before the time arrives.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThen he looked at me more directly.\n\n\"What if I'd said no?\"\n\n\"To a later dinner?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then we'd look for another time. Or I'd ask Greg whether somebody else could handle the setup. You don't have to accept every compromise just because I suggest it.\"\n\nHe sat back.\n\n\"I think I needed to hear that.\"\n\nI smiled a little.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nThe conversation could have ended there. Instead, Danse cleared his throat.\n\n\"There's something else.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"At the yard, people talk.\"\n\n\"About us?\"\n\n\"About your other relationships. About where you go. They act as though knowing your schedule entitles them to an opinion of you.\"\n\nMy jaw tightened.\n\n\"What did they say?\"\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n\"I don't want to repeat it. I told them our private lives weren't their business.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nHe looked uncertain.\n\n\"Was that the right response?\"\n\n\"It was yours. I appreciate it. And if somebody asks me, I'll speak for myself.\"\n\n\"I didn't want to make decisions for you.\"\n\n\"That's one of the reasons I love you.\"\n\nThe words escaped before I could measure them.\n\nHe went still.\n\nThen his face softened.\n\n\"I love you too,\" he said.\n\nI reached across the table. He took my hand.\n\nWe sat together for a few minutes longer, not solving every future argument but making the next one a little less frightening.\n\nBefore I left, Danse pulled a small sheet from the service log and wrote down the evenings he knew he would be at Brasswatch. He crossed out the times that depended on the purifier inspection. I added my own commitments in a different ink.\n\n\"That isn't an order,\" he said when I looked at the page.\n\n\"Good. Because your handwriting is intimidating enough without a rank attached.\"\n\nHe huffed a laugh. \"It is legible.\"\n\n\"Militarily legible.\"\n\nThe pencil paused above Friday. \"I can ask you for time,\" he said. \"I can't reserve you in advance merely because I would like you here.\"\n\n\"And I can tell you when I need a change, instead of assuming you'll accommodate it.\"\n\nHe looked up. \"Precisely.\"\n\nI folded the paper and put it in my pocket. He stopped me by lightly catching my sleeve, then let go when I turned. His expression held the same startled courage I remembered from the first time he'd admitted wanting me near him.\n\n\"One more thing,\" he said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Would you kiss me before you go?\"\n\nI did, slow enough that he could feel I wasn't merely trying to make an argument disappear. His hand rested against the back of my coat. When I pulled away, he looked faintly dazed and deeply annoyed to have been observed in that condition.\n\n\"Report complete?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not remotely,\" he replied, which was the closest thing to flirting he would ever call by its proper name.\n\nAt Starwake, Greg was setting up a small stage with a volunteer crew. The railings had been repaired, and the weather was clear. I helped move the equipment list to a dry board and checked that the new storage space wasn't blocking the walkway.\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"You okay?\"\n\n\"Yes. I need to leave at six.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"Look at you, having boundaries.\"\n\n\"Don't sound so proud.\"\n\n\"I'm impressed. It's rare around here.\"\n\nWe finished the inventory early. I left the rest to the volunteers who had agreed to handle it.\n\nNola caught up with us before I left the pier. She carried the revised roster and wanted to make sure Greg's set ended on time.\n\n\"He said he'd play two songs,\" she told me. \"Somebody's already printed a poster promising three.\"\n\nI looked toward the stage. Greg was helping a volunteer put a cable into its case, unaware that his time had apparently been given away again.\n\n\"Then the poster is wrong,\" I said.\n\nNola nodded. \"I can correct it, but I'd rather he hear from us than from the audience.\"\n\nWe spoke to him together. Greg was disappointed in the organizer, not angry with us. He agreed to play the two songs he'd promised and let Nola correct the schedule. Nobody needed to become the villain of a romantic argument because a festival committee had made an assumption.\n\nI checked my watch. Six o'clock was still possible.\n\nGreg noticed. \"You'd better go. You've got dinner.\"\n\n\"With Danse.\"\n\n\"I know. Tell him I appreciate the workshop's help with the storage brackets.\"\n\nI smiled. We had talked about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory long before either of them had become comfortable working in the same valley. Their politeness wasn't an obligation to like one another. It was a decision to treat the shared work seriously and let me manage my own relationships.\n\nAs I was leaving, a visitor asked whether Greg and I were together. I answered that we cared about each other, then declined to give a schedule or a list of other partners. The visitor looked curious. That wasn't a reason to tell them more.\n\nOn the upper path, I met Tamsin carrying a packet of complaint forms to Brasswatch. She asked whether the new public notice about workers' rights should include the names of people who had filed concerns.\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"A person can choose to speak publicly. The form shouldn't make them do it.\"\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nShe wrote the correction in the margin before we reached the yard.\n\nBy then the bread at Mara's bakery was still warm, and I had time to buy it without making a dramatic entrance. I was glad. I'd promised someone a quiet dinner, and this time I'd planned enough room around the promise to keep it.\n\nOn the way back to Brasswatch, I stopped at Mara's bakery for bread. She handed me a warm loaf and a look that suggested she knew more about the square's gossip than I wanted to imagine.\n\n\"People love a story,\" she said.\n\n\"Even when they don't know the people in it.\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nI carried the bread home.\n\nDanse had cleared the worktable and set out the two mugs again. This time, when he saw me, he smiled.\n\n\"You're early.\"\n\n\"I know. Terrible habit.\"\n\nHe stood.\n\nI put the bread down, went around the table, and kissed him before he could ask whether I had brought the correct fittings.\n\nHe was startled for half a second. Then he returned the kiss with a warmth that made the long day finally feel finished. His hand rested against my cheek, steady and careful.\n\n\"May I ask a question?\" he murmured.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\n\"Will you stay tonight?\"\n\n\"I'd like to.\"\n\nHe held my gaze.\n\n\"Because you want to?\"\n\n\"Because I want to.\"\n\nHe nodded, satisfied.\n\nWe ate dinner after the furnace was banked. Later, we sat on the back steps and listened to the night settle over the yard. There were no new orders, no promises of perfect scheduling, and no need to make another person's absence into proof of anything.\n\nTomorrow I had other plans.\n\nTonight I was here.\n\nBoth were true.","word_count":1786},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-06":{"number":6,"title":"Steel and Scrap","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 6 of 40","content":"The Brotherhood did not arrive the next day.\n\nOr the day after that.\n\nA working transmitter did not mean a vertibird would materialize the moment Danse wanted one.\n\nHaylen had repaired the transmitter twice before the reply finally came through. The first message had been nothing but static and a clipped confirmation code. The second included a revised pickup window, subject to weather and aircraft availability.\n\nDanse wanted to send another request. I could see him composing the words in his head.\n\n“Would it help?” Haylen asked.\n\n“It would remind command that our position remains exposed.”\n\n“They know. I sent the vulnerability report. Another message won't make a rotor turn faster.”\n\nRhys was sorting ammunition by the doorway. “Maybe the Brotherhood should try sending two vertibirds and see if one gets here before dinner.”\n\n“Nobody is flying across a radstorm for your appetite,” Haylen said without looking up.\n\nHe opened his mouth, considered the prospects of winning, and went back to counting.\n\nDanse stared at the message for a long moment. Then he asked Haylen which components still needed shielding before the rain. She told him, and they made a list together.\n\nIt was the first time I saw his command voice become a question instead of an instruction. He was still precise; he had not turned into a man who accepted delays cheerfully. But Haylen knew that radio better than anyone else at the station, and when she said the receiver needed ten minutes without interruption, he gave her fifteen.\n\n“You're getting good at listening,” I told him.\n\nHe glanced at me. “I am attempting to avoid wasting specialist time.”\n\n“Of course you are.”\n\nI didn't say it was romantic. It wasn't, not yet. It was simply a way to stay alive while we waited. The station remained what it had been: three exhausted soldiers, one persistent outsider, and a building held together by scavenged wire, habit, and stubbornness.\n\nI stayed.\n\nAt first I told myself it was because Cambridge had walls.\n\nThen because Haylen needed help repairing the signal amplifier.\n\nThen because the workbench was better than mine.\n\nBy the time I ran out of practical excuses, Danse had started setting aside coffee for me.\n\nIt was terrible coffee.\n\nThat did not seem to matter.\n\nHaylen had left a folded maintenance ticket beside the hot plate. I picked it up while Danse watched the pot like an enemy position.\n\n\"She says the amplifier needs a replacement coil.\"\n\n\"She knows. She is an excellent technician.\"\n\n\"Do you tell her that?\"\n\nHe looked at me as though I'd asked whether he ever thanked a rifle. \"I approve her work.\"\n\n\"That's not quite the same.\"\n\nThe coffee boiled up the side of the pot. I pulled it away from the heat before it could overflow, and Danse went very still.\n\n\"That was under control.\"\n\n\"Sure it was, Paladin.\"\n\nHe took the ticket, read Haylen's notes, and underlined the coil size she had recommended. His pencil hovered over a blank line where the approval usually went.\n\n\"I do not want her to think I doubt her judgment,\" he said.\n\n\"Then don't make her prove a measurement twice just because you weren't the person holding the instrument.\"\n\nThe line of his mouth flattened. He considered the ticket, then wrote APPROVED and added an instruction for Rhys to collect the part rather than interrupt Haylen's repair work. I watched him make the decision without turning it into a parade.\n\n\"You're staring,\" he said.\n\n\"I like knowing how you think.\"\n\n\"That is a dangerous hobby.\"\n\nThe coffee tasted like burned metal. He tried my cup, grimaced, and set it down with a solemnity usually reserved for classified evidence.\n\n\"You're right,\" he said.\n\n\"About the amplifier?\"\n\n\"About the coffee.\"\n\nHe set out another clean mug and asked how I actually liked it. There were easier ways to flirt. I was beginning to understand that Danse didn't trust any of them.\n\n“You boil it too long,” I told him one morning.\n\nHe stood at the small hot plate in the back room, watching a dented pot with tactical suspicion.\n\n“It’s coffee.”\n\n“That is not a defense.”\n\n“It contains caffeine.”\n\n“So does a lot of medicine. I don’t want that in a mug either.”\n\nHe poured two cups.\n\n“You complain every time.”\n\n“And yet you keep giving it to me.”\n\n“You keep drinking it.”\n\nWe looked at each other.\n\nHe had me there.\n\nI took the cup.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“You’re welcome.”\n\nThe exchange should not have felt domestic.\n\nThe station itself prevented that. Rhys was cleaning a rifle at the front desk while muttering about perimeter discipline. Haylen had fallen asleep over a terminal with one cheek pressed against her sleeve. A generator coughed outside. Somewhere in the walls, a radroach scratched with criminal confidence.\n\nStill, standing across from Danse in the yellow morning light, drinking terrible coffee he had made without being asked, I felt something settle.\n\nHe noticed me looking toward Haylen.\n\n“She was up most of the night.”\n\n“So were you.”\n\n“I slept.”\n\n“When?”\n\n“Enough.”\n\n“That is not a time.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You keep unusually detailed track of my sleep schedule.”\n\n“You make it easy by barely having one.”\n\nHe took a drink.\n\nHis face did not react to the coffee at all.\n\nThat might have been the most disturbing thing about him.\n\nAfter breakfast, he put me through firearms drills.\n\nI had not agreed to firearms drills.\n\nApparently remaining within fifteen feet of Paladin Danse for multiple consecutive days constituted implied consent to training.\n\n“Again,” he said.\n\nI lowered Righteous Authority.\n\n“I hit all six targets.”\n\n“Your grouping is inconsistent.”\n\n“They are cans.”\n\n“Today they’re cans. Tomorrow they could be synths.”\n\n“Do synths often stand in neat rows on fence posts?”\n\n“Again.”\n\nI muttered something rude.\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“I said ‘outstanding.’”\n\n“Your sarcasm needs work.”\n\nI fired again.\n\nThe last can jumped from the fence.\n\nDanse inspected the grouping.\n\n“Better.”\n\nI stared at him until he added, “Much better.”\n\n“See? Growth.”\n\n“In you, perhaps.”\n\n“Hopeless.”\n\nHis eyes warmed.\n\nThen a noise came from the street.\n\nThe change in him was instant.\n\nDanse moved between me and the doorway before either of us knew what the sound was. His hand went to his sidearm. His body blocked mine on pure reflex.\n\nA brahmin wandered past the entrance wearing half a shopping cart as luggage.\n\nSilence.\n\nI looked at Danse’s back.\n\nHe slowly lowered his weapon.\n\n“That,” I said, “was terrifying.”\n\n“The animal could have been accompanied.”\n\n“By what? An organized raider dairy operation?”\n\nHe stepped aside.\n\n“You mock caution until the day caution saves your life.”\n\n“I’m not mocking caution.”\n\n“You’re laughing.”\n\n“I’m mocking you.”\n\n“That’s becoming a pattern.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe seemed about to say something else when his attention shifted past me.\n\nRhys stood in the doorway.\n\n“Paladin. Patrol?”\n\nDanse’s expression hardened back into command.\n\n“Five minutes.”\n\nRhys nodded and disappeared.\n\nThe warmth vanished with him.\n\nNot because Danse was pretending around me.\n\nBecause command was another suit of armor, and he wore it whenever someone under him was watching.\n\nI understood that more each day.\n\nThe patrol took us through Cambridge toward an old medical building. Haylen had marked it as a possible source of antiseptics and circuitry. Rhys came with us, limping less than before and resenting me more efficiently.\n\nInside, we found raiders.\n\nThe fight was ugly and close.\n\nAt one point Rhys pushed through a doorway before Danse cleared the second angle. A shotgun blast caught him across the shoulder plate and spun him sideways.\n\nDanse’s response was immediate.\n\nHe put two laser shots through the shooter, dragged Rhys behind cover, and turned on him.\n\n“What the hell were you thinking?”\n\n“I had the room.”\n\n“You had half the room.”\n\n“I saw—”\n\n“You saw what was in front of you. That’s not the same thing.”\n\nRhys’s face tightened.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nDanse checked the wound himself.\n\nHis hands were careful despite the anger.\n\nAfterward, on the walk back, he barely spoke.\n\nRhys went ahead.\n\nI stayed beside Danse.\n\n“You saved him.”\n\n“He shouldn’t have needed saving.”\n\n“People make mistakes.”\n\n“Not all mistakes are survivable.”\n\nThe words came too quickly.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was staring at Rhys’s back.\n\n“Is this about the four names?”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe admission surprised both of us.\n\nWe walked several steps in silence.\n\n“I read them,” I said. “Brach. Worwick. Dawes. Keane.”\n\nDanse stopped.\n\nThe street around us was empty except for weeds moving in the wind.\n\nI expected him to be angry.\n\nInstead he looked tired.\n\n“Keane died here,” he said. “Right outside the station. The others…”\n\nHe trailed off.\n\n“You don’t have to tell me.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nThat mattered.\n\nHe looked toward the station in the distance.\n\n“Maybe I should.”\n\nNot now, his face said.\n\nBut someday.\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe started walking again.\n\nAs we reached the barricade, Danse held the door for me.\n\nAn absurdly ordinary gesture.\n\nI passed him and said, “Thanks.”\n\n“Don’t make anything of it.”\n\n“I wasn’t.”\n\n“You were smiling.”\n\n“I smile sometimes.”\n\n“I’ve noticed.”\n\nHe said it without thinking.\n\nThen his expression changed.\n\nSo did mine.\n\nRhys called from inside before either of us could decide what to do with the sentence.\n\nDanse cleared his throat.\n\n“After you.”\n\nI walked into the station.\n\nBehind me, the door shut with a heavy steel click.\n\nThe sound felt suspiciously like a line being crossed.\n\nLiving at the station—because apparently that was what I was doing now—taught me the shape of Danse’s days.\n\nHe woke before everyone else.\n\nI discovered this because I woke early once to the soft clink of free weights in what had once been an evidence room. Danse was inside in a sleeveless undershirt, counting repetitions under his breath with the grim focus of a man personally offended by fatigue.\n\nI leaned against the doorway.\n\n“Do you ever do anything casually?”\n\nHe lowered the bar.\n\n“You’re awake.”\n\n“Sharp observation.”\n\n“It’s 0500.”\n\n“That explains why I hate the world.”\n\nHe sat up and reached for a towel.\n\n“Physical readiness is part of my duty.”\n\n“You could be physically ready at seven.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Six-thirty?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Compromise means both people suffer.”\n\n“That is not what compromise means.”\n\nI stepped into the room and examined the collection of scavenged weights. Several were old engine parts threaded onto a steel pipe.\n\n“You made these.”\n\n“Rhys did.”\n\nThat surprised me enough that I looked toward the sleeping area.\n\nDanse noticed.\n\n“He is more resourceful than his disposition suggests.”\n\n“That is the kindest insult I’ve ever heard.”\n\n“It was not an insult.”\n\n“Of course not.”\n\nHe gave me the look I was beginning to collect.\n\nA few minutes later he showed me how to brace the improvised bar so it would not roll off its stand. Then he corrected my grip. Then my stance. Then, because he was Danse, he turned the entire interaction into a safety briefing.\n\n“You are taking this very seriously.”\n\n“Injury is preventable.”\n\n“So is boredom.”\n\n“Your priorities concern me.”\n\nBut he was smiling when he said it.\n\nAfterward we sat on the floor sharing a bottle of purified water.\n\nThe station was still quiet. Haylen’s transmitter clicked softly in the next room. Rhys snored with an aggression that seemed personally directed at sleep.\n\nDanse leaned his head back against the wall.\n\nFor once, no helmet. No rifle in his hands. No one asking him for a decision.\n\n“You look tired,” I said.\n\n“I’m fine.”\n\n“Your favorite lie.”\n\nHis eyes opened.\n\n“I don’t lie.”\n\n“You reorganized three ammunition crates at midnight.”\n\n“That needed to be done.”\n\n“They were already organized.”\n\n“Poorly.”\n\nI let the silence sit.\n\nEventually he looked away.\n\n“Sleep has been difficult since the expedition.”\n\nThe expedition.\n\nNot yet the names. Not yet the details.\n\nBut it was something.\n\nI passed him the water.\n\n“You don’t have to tell me.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nThat seemed to surprise him too.\n\nMost people, given a crack in armor, tried to pry it wider.\n\nI knew enough about damaged things to leave pressure off a fracture until it was ready to bear weight.\n\nDanse took another drink.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“Not asking.”\n\nI shrugged.\n\n“When you want me to know, you’ll tell me.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\nSomething in the room changed—not romance, not yet. Something quieter and more important.\n\nTrust did not always arrive as a confession.\n\nSometimes it arrived as the decision that one would be safe when it came.\n","word_count":2075},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-20":{"number":20,"title":"Permission to Speak Freely","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 20 of 40","content":"Danse kissed me by accident.\n\nNot literally.\n\nPaladin Danse did very little literally by accident.\n\nBut the first kiss happened because neither of us had apparently planned beyond standing too close in a corridor.\n\nThe evening began with a debriefing.\n\nWe had spent the day clearing a raider position from an old research facility. Nothing unusual. Nobody seriously injured. Valuable technical documents recovered. By Brotherhood standards, an excellent mission.\n\nDanse dismissed the squad and asked me to stay.\n\nI stayed.\n\nHe closed the briefing-room door.\n\nThat was new.\n\n“What’s wrong?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You closed the door.”\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\n“You’re standing like you’re about to court-martial me.”\n\n“I’m not.”\n\n“Are you sure?”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Can I finish?”\n\n“You haven’t started.”\n\nDanse closed his eyes.\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\nWhen he opened them again, the command was gone.\n\nNerves remained.\n\n“I wanted to discuss what happened during the storm.”\n\n“We slept.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Scandalous.”\n\n“That’s not what I mean.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe paced once.\n\n“I don’t want you to think I took advantage of the situation.”\n\nI stared at him.\n\n“You held me while we slept.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I was there.”\n\n“I’m aware.”\n\n“I could have moved.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Danse.”\n\nHe stopped.\n\n“You didn’t take advantage of anything.”\n\nHis shoulders eased.\n\n“Good.”\n\n“But that’s not actually what you’re worried about.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“You do this too often.”\n\n“Do what?”\n\n“Make me say the part I’m trying not to say.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you’ll build a bunker around it otherwise.”\n\n“That’s not fair.”\n\n“Accurate, though.”\n\nHe looked away.\n\nThe room hummed with ventilation.\n\nFinally he said, “Things are changing between us.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I don’t know what to do with that.”\n\n“You could try asking what I want.”\n\nDanse looked genuinely startled.\n\n“What do you want?”\n\nThere it was.\n\nSimple question.\n\nTerrifying answer.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n“You.”\n\nHis breathing stopped.\n\nNot forever. Just long enough to make me notice.\n\n“That’s…”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“Not an answer.”\n\n“It is extremely an answer.”\n\n“I meant specifically.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“You want tactical detail?”\n\nHe realized how that sounded.\n\nColor rose into his face.\n\n“I mean—”\n\n“I know.”\n\nI rescued him because I was not cruel.\n\nUsually.\n\n“I want to spend time with you when we’re not working. I want you to stop pretending holding my hand is an administrative error. I want to see what this is without acting like wanting anything automatically makes it dangerous.”\n\nDanse stared at me.\n\n“I don’t know if I can give you that.”\n\nThe honesty hurt less than a false promise would have.\n\n“Because of regulations?”\n\n“Partly.”\n\n“Cutler?”\n\nHis eyes flickered.\n\n“Partly.”\n\n“And the rest?”\n\nHe looked toward the Brotherhood emblem on the wall.\n\n“I’m good at being a soldier.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I understand expectations. Orders. Objectives. This…”\n\nHis hand moved helplessly between us.\n\n“I’m not good at this.”\n\nI thought of him making terrible coffee for me. Tightening my vertibird harness. Writing maintenance notes. Sitting up all night with dead men’s lockers. Holding my hand through a radstorm.\n\n“You are,” I said.\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“You’re just not good at talking about it.”\n\nThat surprised a laugh from him.\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“Definitely.”\n\nI moved one step closer.\n\nDanse did not retreat.\n\n“Permission to speak freely?” I asked.\n\nHis mouth softened.\n\n“Granted.”\n\n“I think about kissing you.”\n\nEvery trace of military composure left his face.\n\nI had never seen anything more charming.\n\n“Oh.”\n\n“That’s all you have?”\n\n“I wasn’t prepared for that.”\n\n“You asked for specifics.”\n\n“I regret it.”\n\n“No, you don’t.”\n\nHis eyes dropped to my mouth.\n\n“No,” he admitted. “I don’t.”\n\nThe space between us became very small.\n\n“Danse?”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“Do you think about kissing me?”\n\nHis answer was barely audible.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI waited.\n\nSo did he.\n\nThen we both leaned in.\n\nOur foreheads bumped.\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse swore softly.\n\n“Outstanding,” I whispered.\n\n“Don’t.”\n\nI was still smiling when he tried again.\n\nThe kiss was careful.\n\nThat was the first thing I noticed.\n\nNot timid exactly. Controlled. Like he was afraid of assuming too much. His hand came up to my jaw and stopped there, warm against my skin.\n\nI leaned closer.\n\nControl became something softer.\n\nHis mouth moved against mine with a quiet hunger that seemed to surprise him as much as it did me.\n\nWhen we separated, Danse kept his forehead against mine.\n\nNeither of us spoke.\n\nThen he said, “This complicates things.”\n\nI laughed directly against his mouth.\n\n“Of course that’s your first thought.”\n\n“It’s true.”\n\n“So is the fact that you kissed me.”\n\n“You kissed me too.”\n\n“Shared responsibility.”\n\n“That sounds suspiciously like something I taught you.”\n\n“See? Good officer.”\n\nHis thumb moved once along my cheek.\n\nThe gesture was so tender it hurt.\n\n“I don’t know what happens next,” he said.\n\n“Neither do I.”\n\nHe looked uneasy.\n\nI added, “We don’t need a plan tonight.”\n\nDanse stared at me like I had suggested abandoning gravity.\n\nThen slowly, reluctantly, he smiled.\n\n“One night.”\n\n“One night.”\n\nHe kissed me again.\n\nThis time we did not bump foreheads.\n\nThe problem with kissing someone I had been circling for weeks was that afterward every ordinary interaction developed a second meaning.\n\nDanse handed me ammunition.\n\nMy brain: *his hand was on my face last night.*\n\nDanse asked whether I had checked the perimeter.\n\nMy brain: *his mouth was on mine.*\n\nDanse leaned over my shoulder to inspect a map.\n\nMy brain stopped contributing useful information entirely.\n\n“This route,” he said.\n\n“What?”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“The northern route.”\n\n“Right.”\n\n“You weren’t listening.”\n\n“I was distracted.”\n\n“By what?”\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe understood.\n\nHis ears turned red.\n\n“Oh.”\n\n“Yeah.”\n\nWe both looked at the map.\n\nIt became the most interesting map in the Commonwealth.\n\nFinally Danse cleared his throat.\n\n“We should establish expectations.”\n\nI turned slowly.\n\n“Of course you want a briefing for this.”\n\n“This is not a briefing.”\n\n“Are there objectives?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Rules of engagement?”\n\nHis expression became deeply pained.\n\n“Please stop.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe tension broke enough for him to breathe.\n\nDanse sat on the edge of the desk.\n\n“I’m serious.”\n\n“So am I.”\n\nHe folded his hands.\n\n“I don’t have much experience with…”\n\nHe gestured between us as though romance were a hazardous material.\n\n“This.”\n\n“I guessed.”\n\n“That obvious?”\n\n“You kissed me by walking into my face.”\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“I had hoped we were never going to discuss that.”\n\n“We will discuss it at our wedding.”\n\nDanse’s eyes opened so quickly I nearly apologized.\n\nI did apologize.\n\n“Too much. Sorry.”\n\n“No, I—”\n\nHe looked away.\n\n“It surprised me.”\n\n“Forget I said it.”\n\n“I don’t want to.”\n\nThat silenced me.\n\nDanse stared at his hands.\n\n“I’m not promising anything I can’t give. I don’t know where this goes.”\n\n“Neither do I.”\n\n“But I would like to find out.”\n\nThe careful sincerity of it went straight through me.\n\nI stepped between his knees.\n\n“Okay.”\n\nHis hands settled at my waist and stopped there, giving me every opportunity to move.\n\nI didn’t.\n\n“What do you need?” he asked.\n\nThe question was pure Danse. Not *what are the rules*, not *what am I allowed to do*, but what would keep the person in front of him safe.\n\n“Honesty.”\n\nHe nodded.\n\n“No disappearing into duty every time something scares you.”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“That is very specific.”\n\n“Experience.”\n\n“I’ll try.”\n\n“And you?”\n\nDanse thought.\n\n“Patience.”\n\n“With you?”\n\n“With me. With the Brotherhood. With the fact that I may not know how to balance both immediately.”\n\nI ran my thumb across the seam of his uniform sleeve, carefully away from the fresh tear he'd been pretending not to notice.\n\n\"There's something else you should know before we decide what this is,\" I said.\n\nHe straightened without stepping away. \"I'm listening.\"\n\n\"I practice ethical non-monogamy. I prefer polyamory. It means I may love more than one person, and I don't hide that from anyone I'm involved with.\"\n\nFor a moment I could practically hear him searching his training for an applicable regulation.\n\n\"Would that mean I was expected to be involved with your other partners?\" he asked.\n\n\"No. Nobody is automatically part of anybody else's relationship. We talk about what we each want, what we can consent to, and how we handle time.\"\n\nHe looked relieved, then embarrassed at the relief.\n\n\"I have no experience with that arrangement,\" he said.\n\n\"That's allowed.\"\n\n\"I would need to ask questions.\"\n\n\"Please do.\"\n\n\"And I could decide it isn't suitable for me?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nHe regarded me seriously. \"Then I would like to know what a date would actually involve.\"\n\nI laughed, not because the question was foolish, but because he had somehow made romance sound like a reconnaissance briefing.\n\n\"Tea,\" I said. \"Maybe something edible. A walk without patrol orders.\"\n\n\"Reasonable conditions.\"\n\n\"And a kiss if we both want one.\"\n\nHe touched my waist again, gently enough that I had to choose to move closer. I did. When our mouths met, the hallway and its reports ceased to be useful measures of anything. He was still the most serious man I knew, and the shy little smile afterward nearly undid me.\n\nI rested my hands on his shoulders.\n\n“I can do patience.”\n\nHis expression softened.\n\n“Can you?”\n\n“One more thing,” I said.\n\nDanse’s expression became wary. “There’s more?”\n\n“Public affection.”\n\nHe looked as if I had proposed treason.\n\n“What about it?”\n\n“I don’t need a performance. I also don’t want you acting like touching me is classified information.”\n\n“That seems reasonable.”\n\n“Say it without looking at the door.”\n\nHis gaze snapped back to mine.\n\n“I wasn’t.”\n\n“You were measuring escape distance.”\n\n“I was assessing privacy.”\n\n“Very romantic.”\n\nHe rubbed a hand over the back of his neck. “I’ve never done this.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I mean any of it. Not seriously.”\n\nThe admission softened me.\n\n“Then we learn.”\n\n“Together?”\n\n“That is generally how relationships work.”\n\n“I’m aware of the theory.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse looked relieved to hear it.\n\n“No.”\n\nHe laughed.\n\n“But I can practice.”\n\n“That will have to do.”\n\nI kissed him.\n\nThis time he did not bump foreheads, freeze, or apologize.\n\nHe simply kissed me back, careful at first and then with growing confidence.\n\nWhen we separated, he rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“No wedding jokes for at least a week.”\n\n“Three days.”\n\n“Five.”\n\n“Four.”\n\n“Agreed.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nWe did eventually leave the briefing room.\n\nDanse opened the door, checked the corridor, then stopped.\n\nI waited.\n\nHe looked at my hand.\n\nThen, with the expression of someone testing a dangerous new piece of equipment, he took it.\n\nTwo scribes rounded the corner.\n\nDanse almost released me.\n\nAlmost.\n\nThey saluted. He returned it with his free hand.\n\nNeither commented.\n\nBy the time they passed, his grip had gone painfully tight.\n\n“Still alive?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Good first field trial.”\n\nHe exhaled something suspiciously like a laugh.\n\nThere it was: our first romantic negotiation.\n\nNaturally, Danse had turned it into a treaty.\n","word_count":1800},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-33":{"number":33,"title":"Heart and Mind","subtitle":"What the Armor Leaves Behind · Chapter 33 of 40","content":"Danse asked me whether I believed he had a soul.\n\nThere are questions I answer quickly because hesitation becomes its own answer.\n\n“Yes,” I said.\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“That was immediate.”\n\n“You wanted me to think longer?”\n\n“I wanted honesty.”\n\n“You got it.”\n\nWe were sitting outside Bravo at sunset, backs against the concrete wall, rifles within reach. The Commonwealth stretched around us in dead grass and autumn light.\n\nDanse picked at the edge of the red scarf he now wore almost every day.\n\n“What is a soul?” he asked.\n\nI laughed softly.\n\n“You waited until after I answered to define terms?”\n\n“I’m serious.”\n\n“So am I. I don’t know.”\n\nHe looked dissatisfied.\n\n“Then how can you say I have one?”\n\n“Because whatever people mean by it, they usually mean the part that chooses, hurts, loves, regrets, hopes.”\n\n“That could all be neural programming.”\n\n“So could mine.”\n\n“You’re biological.”\n\n“Biology is programming with meat.”\n\nDanse made a face.\n\n“That is revolting.”\n\n“Accurate, though.”\n\nHe almost smiled.\n\nThen the worry returned.\n\n“I spent years believing synths couldn’t make real decisions.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“If I accept that I can, then…”\n\n“Then Nick can.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Other synths can.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And that means you were wrong.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe word cost him.\n\nI let it settle.\n\nDanse looked at the horizon.\n\n“I said things.”\n\n“You did.”\n\n“I would have killed people.”\n\nHe ran his thumb over the edge of a metal buckle lying beside us. It had come loose from a worn pack, and he had been meaning to repair it for days.\n\n\"I want to say I would have recognized a person if one had been standing in front of me,\" he said. \"But Nick was standing in front of me.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And I judged him by what I believed he was made of.\"\n\nI wanted to make it easier. There was no honest way to do that.\n\n\"Would you tell him?\" I asked.\n\nDanse stared at the buckle. \"I don't know if he'd want to hear it.\"\n\n\"Then that's the first question. Not whether apologizing would make you feel better.\"\n\nHe made a low sound of agreement. His palm was rough where an old callus met the edge of his finger. I let my hand rest beside his on the ground without insisting he take it.\n\n\"I still know how to fix a pack,\" he said after a while. \"I don't know how to repair that.\"\n\n\"One doesn't replace the other.\"\n\nHe tested the buckle's spring and set it back in the bag. The small deliberate movement felt more like him than another declaration of transformation. He was allowed to be wrong about people and still responsible for what he did next.\n\nWhen he finally took my hand, he squeezed once. The Commonwealth was still full of decisions he had to make for himself. I loved him enough not to claim that choosing me settled the rest.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis eyes closed.\n\n“You’re not going to soften this for me.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you deserve better than comfort built on a lie.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nSomething in his expression broke open.\n\n“That’s why I trust you.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\nDanse continued, slowly.\n\n“I don’t know if what I feel is friendship, attachment, or some anomaly in how I was made.”\n\nI did not interrupt.\n\n“But I know I feel closer to you than anyone I’ve ever known.”\n\nThe sunset blurred slightly.\n\nI blinked hard.\n\nHe looked alarmed.\n\n“Did I say something wrong?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“You’re crying.”\n\n“Observant.”\n\n“I’m serious.”\n\n“So am I.”\n\nI wiped my face.\n\n“You’re allowed to say things that hurt in a good way.”\n\n“That makes no sense.”\n\n“Emotion is inefficient.”\n\n“Terrible system.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHe smiled.\n\nI took his hand.\n\n“I feel closer to you too.”\n\nDanse looked down at our fingers.\n\n“Even now?”\n\n“Especially now.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because now you’re not hiding behind certainty every second.”\n\nHis expression became mock-offended.\n\n“I did not hide.”\n\n“You wore power armor emotionally.”\n\n“That metaphor remains inaccurate.”\n\n“And yet you know exactly what I mean.”\n\nHe sighed.\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\nAfter a while he said, “I’m afraid.”\n\n“Of us?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHis head turned.\n\n“Good?”\n\n“It matters enough to scare you.”\n\n“That is an unhealthy standard.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\nHe considered.\n\n“I’m also afraid you’ll realize this is too much.”\n\nI lifted my head.\n\n“Danse.”\n\n“I’m serious. The Brotherhood, the synth issue, my lack of any clear future—”\n\nI kissed him.\n\nHe stopped talking.\n\nWhen I pulled back, he frowned.\n\n“That was not a response.”\n\n“It was an efficient one.”\n\n“You’re impossible.”\n\n“You like me.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe answer came instantly.\n\nWe both froze.\n\nDanse looked surprised.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\n“I suppose I do.”\n\n“You suppose?”\n\nHe leaned in.\n\n“I’m revising the statement.”\n\nThe kiss was warm and slow beneath a sky gone purple over the Commonwealth.\n\nWhen we separated, he whispered, “I love you.”\n\nNo speech.\n\nNo tactical framing.\n\nNo permission requested.\n\nJust the truth.\n\nI touched his cheek.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\nDanse closed his eyes.\n\nFor once, he did not ask whether it was real.\n\nSaying love out loud did not solve the soul question.\n\nIt made it less lonely.\n\nThe next morning Danse was quieter than usual. Not withdrawn exactly. He moved through breakfast as if he had spent the night dismantling every word he had said and inspecting the pieces for Institute markings.\n\nI let him make the coffee.\n\nThis was a mistake, but a loving one.\n\nHe set a mug in front of me.\n\n“Drinkable?” I asked.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nIt was not.\n\nI drank anyway.\n\nDanse watched.\n\n“You hate it.”\n\n“I love you.”\n\n“That is not the same statement.”\n\n“Today it is.”\n\nHis eyes dropped to his own mug.\n\nThere it was.\n\nI reached across the table.\n\n“What are you doing?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You reorganized the spoon drawer at six in the morning.”\n\n“It was disordered.”\n\n“You alphabetized canned food.”\n\n“By contents.”\n\n“That is not how alphabetizing works.”\n\nHis mouth almost moved.\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\n“Tell me.”\n\nDanse stared at our fingers.\n\n“What if saying it changes how I feel?”\n\nI waited.\n\nHe forced himself to continue.\n\n“What if I become dependent on you? What if I start making decisions because I’m afraid you’ll leave?”\n\n“That could happen.”\n\nHe looked up.\n\n“Again with that answer.”\n\n“I’m not going to promise love has no failure modes.”\n\nHe leaned back, unhappy but listening.\n\n“I could become afraid of losing you. You could become afraid of losing me. We could hurt each other. We could get stupid. We could stay together too long if it stops being good.”\n\n“That is a deeply unromantic speech.”\n\n“Wait for the ending.”\n\nHe crossed his arms.\n\n“The alternative is never choosing anything because it could go wrong.”\n\nDanse looked at the table.\n\n“I did that with people for years.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“It did not prevent loss.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“And it did not prevent attachment.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis expression turned annoyed.\n\n“You’re enjoying this.”\n\n“Immensely.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you are doing my argument for me.”\n\nDanse sighed.\n\nI stood and moved around the table.\n\nHe opened his knees enough for me to stand between them.\n\n“Love isn’t proof that you’re human,” I said.\n\nHis eyes sharpened.\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Nick could love someone and still be metal. Amos could love someone and still be a ghoul. A person who never loves anyone is still a person.”\n\n“Then what does it prove?”\n\n“That you love me.”\n\nHe blinked.\n\n“That’s all?”\n\n“That’s enormous.”\n\nDanse looked at me for a long time.\n\nThen his hands settled around my waist.\n\n“I dislike arguments where the conclusion is obvious only after you say it.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You do that often.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Stop agreeing with me.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe pulled me closer.\n\nA knock sounded on Bravo’s outer door.\n\nWe both froze.\n\nDanse’s hands left me instantly, body turning toward the rifle by the table.\n\nOld reflexes.\n\nI reached it first and passed it to him.\n\nWe moved together through the corridor.\n\nThe visitor was a trader from the eastern settlement carrying a broken pistol and a sack of potatoes.\n\n“Sorry,” she said when the door opened. “Mara said Danse might fix this.”\n\nDanse stared at the pistol.\n\nThen at me.\n\n“Apparently I have business hours now.”\n\n“You have no sign.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nThe trader handed him the weapon.\n\nHe repaired the feed mechanism at the table while she told us about a radstag herd moving through the area. She paid him in potatoes despite his attempt to refuse half of them.\n\nWhen she left, Danse looked at the bag.\n\n“I like potatoes.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Add it to the list.”\n\nHe did.\n\nBefore the pencil touched the paper again, Danse stared at the word for a long time.\n\n“I used to think love was mostly a liability,” he said.\n\n“Mostly?”\n\n“Attachment creates exploitable weaknesses.”\n\n“That sounds like a field manual.”\n\n“It might have been.”\n\n“And Cutler?”\n\nHis expression softened painfully.\n\n“An exception I refused to classify.”\n\n“Haylen?”\n\n“Another.”\n\n“Me?”\n\nDanse looked up.\n\n“You are becoming statistically inconvenient.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe did not.\n\n“I mean it. At some point a rule with enough exceptions is a bad rule.”\n\nMy smile faded.\n\nThere it was: the same lesson Amos had started months ago, reaching somewhere deeper now that Danse himself had become the category his doctrine condemned.\n\n“You don’t have to solve love tonight,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“That sounded almost relaxed.”\n\n“Don’t ruin it.”\n\nHe finally lowered the pencil.\n\nDanse turned the pencil between his fingers.\n\n“Do you think loving someone proves anything about being human?”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because I don’t think humanity gets to own every good thing.”\n\nHe went very still.\n\nI touched the paper between us.\n\n“You don’t need to qualify for personhood by imitating the best version of a human. Humans are generous and cruel and brave and cowardly and everything in between.”\n\n“And synths?”\n\n“I imagine they get to be complicated too.”\n\nA slow breath left him.\n\n“That is less comforting than I expected.”\n\n“Good. Comforting lies are overrated.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThe pencil hovered once, then settled. Danse had spent months asking whether his feelings were manufactured. He was finally beginning to ask what he intended to do with them instead.\n\nThen beneath it he wrote LOVE.\n\nI watched the pencil pause.\n\nHe added, after a moment:\n\nEVEN WHEN IT IS INCONVENIENT.\n\n“That’s a terrible definition,” I said.\n\n“I wasn’t defining it.”\n\n“What, then?”\n\n“Recording field conditions.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nDanse pulled me into his lap.\n\n“I love you,” he said again.\n\nThe second time sounded different.\n\nLess like a discovery.\n\nMore like a choice he had decided to keep making.\n","word_count":1770},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-47":{"number":47,"title":"A Practical Kind of Courage","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"The trainee asked Danse why the workshop safety handbook had a section titled IDENTIFY UNTRUSTWORTHY PERSONNEL.\n\nThe question landed in the room like a dropped wrench.\n\nRenn was an adult apprentice from a border settlement who had come to Brasswatch for a short training placement. They were learning to maintain water pumps and repair simple metal fittings. They had proved patient with measurements, careful with tools, and completely uninterested in the idea that a former Paladin's reputation should end a conversation.\n\nDanse looked at the page.\n\n\"That section was copied from an older manual.\"\n\n\"Why is it still here?\" Renn asked.\n\nHe didn't answer immediately.\n\nI was at the next table checking the revised payment records. Ash was working in the adjoining room, where the private door now locked from the inside. They could hear the discussion, but nobody had asked them to participate.\n\nRenn tapped the page.\n\n\"It says unfamiliar people should be reported until their origin is confirmed. That sounds like a reason to watch everyone who comes through the Threadways.\"\n\nDanse rubbed a hand across his jaw.\n\n\"It was written for a military environment.\"\n\n\"Is the workshop a military environment?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why are we teaching it?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe met my eyes, then looked back at the trainee.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nRenn blinked.\n\n\"I am?\"\n\n\"Yes. That section doesn't belong in this handbook.\"\n\nIt could have ended there, with a simple correction and everyone returning to work.\n\nBut Danse was staring at the page with the expression of someone who had discovered an old voice speaking through his own handwriting.\n\n\"I used to believe those procedures were necessary,\" he said. \"I enforced them.\"\n\nRenn waited.\n\n\"That doesn't make them correct,\" Danse continued. \"And it doesn't make the people affected by them responsible for making me feel better about changing.\"\n\nI set down the ledger.\n\nIt wasn't a polished apology. It was better.\n\nRenn looked at him.\n\n\"Then can we write a new rule?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThey pulled a blank sheet from the drawer.\n\nThe new section would concern actual safety behavior: handling hazardous materials, keeping exit routes clear, documenting threats without profiling visitors, asking consent before examining personal belongings, and protecting private information unless there was a specific, immediate danger.\n\nIt would not require anyone to disclose a homeworld, identity, or past allegiance merely to receive repair service.\n\nI helped check the wording.\n\n\"How do we make sure people actually follow it?\" Renn asked.\n\n\"Training,\" Danse said.\n\n\"And consequences,\" I added.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Consequences.\"\n\nBy noon, we had revised three pages.\n\nThe work revealed other old assumptions. One instruction required the person in charge to approve every emergency exit. That made sense for a locked armory and no sense for a public workshop where people might need to leave during a fire.\n\nAnother said workers should ignore a task interruption until given permission to stop. That was how injuries happened.\n\nRenn crossed it out.\n\nDanse watched them.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" he asked.\n\n\"At the old handbook? Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\nRenn thought about it.\n\n\"A little. But mostly I want to know whether this place will be different.\"\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Then judge the changes we make.\"\n\nI could see how hard it was for him not to ask for forgiveness, not to explain his old reasoning until it sounded noble. He let the discomfort stay.\n\nBefore the test, Ash asked to read the revised handbook in the private workroom.\n\nRenn carried them a clean copy and waited outside while Ash looked through it. When they returned, two sections had been marked with dark pencil.\n\n\"You changed the question about where people come from,\" Ash said. \"But you still ask whether someone has a lawful reason to be here.\"\n\nDanse frowned.\n\n\"That line concerns workshop access.\"\n\n\"Then say workshop access. People can have an appointment or need an urgent repair without proving they belong in the valley.\"\n\nI watched Danse look at the sentence as though it had appeared on the page while he wasn't paying attention.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\nHe crossed it out.\n\nRenn added a practical substitute: *Ask what service is needed and whether it can be provided safely. For hazardous equipment, explain specific training and access restrictions without demanding personal history.*\n\nAsh nodded.\n\n\"Better. And the complaint process?\"\n\nDanse showed them the line that directed complaints to the workshop supervisor.\n\n\"That's you,\" Ash said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"What happens if the complaint is about you?\"\n\nI saw the discomfort move across his face.\n\n\"Then it cannot stop with me.\"\n\nWe wrote a second path to the independent steward and included where to leave a sealed note. The procedure would be posted at the entrance, not hidden in the handbook after forty pages.\n\nDanse put the pencil down.\n\n\"I thought I was making the rules clearer.\"\n\n\"You are,\" Renn said. \"Because people can question them.\"\n\nRenn pulled a pump gasket from the work tray and held it up against the old handbook's illustration.\n\n\"That diagram is wrong too,\" they said.\n\nDanse leaned over the page. \"The orientation is reversed.\"\n\n\"Then why does it say the apprentice is responsible if the part fails?\"\n\nFor a moment he looked like the Paladin who could have ended the conversation by repeating the regulation. Instead he found the original drawing and placed it next to the real gasket.\n\n\"Because the text was copied without being checked,\" he answered. \"That is our failure.\"\n\nRenn took a pencil and drew a corrected arrow. Ash, who had been sorting replacement seals, pointed out that a trainee using that diagram could damage the pump and still be blamed for following instructions.\n\n\"Add that to the revision history,\" Ash said.\n\nDanse did. He kept both versions and dated the correction rather than quietly erasing evidence that the first had been wrong.\n\nI walked over with a box of tools and found them arguing about whether the independent safety reviewer should be able to stop the class. All three agreed that they should. Then they had to work out how the reviewer would get a message to the instructor when the machinery was loud.\n\nThe discussion was long, practical and sometimes irritating. Nobody praised Danse for finally listening. At the end he moved the emergency signal cord to a height the trainees could actually reach.\n\nHe caught me looking at him. \"It isn't perfect.\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"It's something people can use.\"\n\nHis smile was small and quick. I liked it more than a victory speech.\n\nAsh returned the copy without praising him.\n\nThey didn't owe him that.\n\nWhen the emergency exercise began, I noticed Danse left the marked copy on the front table where anyone could see the revisions. The old habit would have been to lock away a flawed procedure until it was perfect.\n\nToday the process itself was public, with a date beside the next review.\n\nIt was one more way this workshop differed from the place he'd once called home.\n\nThat afternoon we tested the new emergency procedure.\n\nPip played the role of someone arriving with a box of loose fittings and no appointment. Edda tested the public entrance. Renn simulated a small electrical fault using a disconnected training board. Ash chose to observe from the private workroom with the door closed.\n\nDanse did not order anyone to reveal where they came from.\n\nHe asked where help was needed.\n\nWhen Renn reported the simulated fault, the group used the accessible exit and left the tools behind. The test wasn't perfect. Someone had to move a cart out of the way. The alarm cord was too high for one visitor. We wrote both problems down.\n\n\"Again tomorrow,\" Danse said.\n\nRenn smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nAfter the shift, I found Danse alone in the storage shed looking at the old handbook. He had separated the discarded pages from those still useful. The paper smelled of oil and dust.\n\n\"Will you burn it?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"I want to keep one copy as an example of what not to teach.\"\n\n\"With notes?\"\n\n\"With notes.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Does it feel strange?\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nHe looked down at the title.\n\n\"There was a time when I thought courage meant obeying the right order despite fear. Later I thought it meant refusing an order that was wrong. Today I had to admit that I was still teaching part of the wrong order without noticing.\"\n\n\"That's a different kind of courage.\"\n\n\"Not an exciting one.\"\n\n\"Most useful courage isn't.\"\n\nHe smiled at that.\n\nWe watched the evening settle over Brasswatch Yard. Workers were closing the safe storage shed, and the hand pump was being checked one last time before the night rota began.\n\n\"Do you think Renn will come back tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\n\"Probably. You paid them, listened, and let them correct you.\"\n\n\"That seems like a low standard.\"\n\n\"It's a beginning.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I want to build something people can trust.\"\n\n\"Then keep asking them what works.\"\n\nHe turned toward me.\n\n\"Do you trust me?\"\n\nThe question was quiet enough that I almost missed it.\n\n\"I trust you to tell me the truth about what you're trying to change,\" I said. \"And I trust myself to tell you when something still hurts. I don't need to pretend the past is harmless to love you now.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\nThen he lifted my hand to his lips.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not calling it finished.\"\n\nI leaned close. He kissed me slowly, with none of the stiffness that had once made him hold every tender gesture like a military inspection. His hand rested gently against my cheek, and I smiled into the kiss.\n\n\"Come home,\" I whispered.\n\nHe nodded.\n\nBefore the workshop closed, Ash found a line that still made them uneasy.\n\n“The complaint process goes to the shift supervisor,” they said. “What happens if the complaint is about the shift supervisor?”\n\nDanse took the page back. He had written it as though the person in authority could always be trusted to examine their own conduct. I saw the moment he recognized the old Brotherhood assumption beneath the neat wording.\n\n“Then there must be another reviewer,” he said.\n\n“Who?”\n\n“Someone selected by the workers.”\n\n“Not appointed by you?”\n\n“No.”\n\nRenn looked up from the tool drawer. “Can the trainee ask for an outside witness if the whole workshop has been involved?”\n\nDanse hesitated. “That would complicate a timely investigation.”\n\n“That is sometimes the point,” Ash said.\n\nHe crossed out the line and replaced it with a process that named the independent reviewer, the right to bring a witness and the duty to record evidence without publishing someone's personal history. It did not promise every complaint would be upheld. It promised that a complaint could be heard without going straight back to the person it concerned.\n\nWhen he showed me the revised page, the corner was stained with graphite where he'd erased his first answer.\n\n“You're making a mess of your own handbook,” I said.\n\n“So I am.”\n\n“You okay with that?”\n\nHe gave a faint, reluctant smile. “The original was defective. I would have rejected a component in the same condition.”\n\nI held his gaze for a moment. He was still the man who thought in procedures; now he was finally letting people test them.\n\nWe left the old handbook on the table, marked for revision.\n\nThe following morning, Renn arrived with two new questions and a better drawing of the emergency exit.\n\nDanse greeted them at the door.\n\n\"Good morning,\" he said. \"What have I missed?\"\n\nRenn grinned.\n\n\"Plenty.\"\n\nHe held the door open.\n\n\"Then let's get to work.\"","word_count":1939}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="what-the-armor-leaves-behind");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=85411;})();
